const PARTS$1 = { "1": "Part I · The President (Art. VII)", "2": "Part II · Congress and the Budget", "3": "Part III · Judiciary and JBC (Art. VIII)", "4": "Part IV · Commissions and CHR (Art. IX)" };
const D = /* @__PURE__ */ JSON.parse(`[{"id":"marcos","part":1,"n":1,"name":"Marcos v. Manglapus","cite":"G.R. No. 88211 · Sept. 15, 1989","hook":"Leftover power keeps the peace.","rule":"The President has residual (leftover) powers beyond those listed, and may stop a citizen's return when national security requires it, as long as there is a factual basis.","mem":["“The executive power shall be vested in the President of the Philippines.” — Art. VII, Sec. 1"],"doc":["Executive power is **more than the sum** of the listed powers. The President has **residual powers** to protect the general welfare.","The **right to return** is different from the right to travel. It may give way to national security.","**TEST:** Courts only check for **grave abuse of discretion** — was there a factual basis?"],"drill":"A former President living in exile announces he will fly home next week. Intelligence reports show armed loyalists plan to seize two military camps when he arrives. The President orders him barred \\"until the threat ends.\\" No law gives this power. He says the order is void because the power is not in the Constitution. Is he correct?","alac":["No. The order barring his return is valid.","The Constitution vests the executive power in the President. Jurisprudence provides that this power is not limited to the listed powers; the President has residual powers to keep peace and order. The right to return may be limited for national security, and courts only check for grave abuse of discretion.","Here, no provision expressly allows the ban, but it falls within residual power to keep the peace. There is a factual basis: armed loyalists plan to seize camps. The order is also temporary. There is no grave abuse.","Therefore, the order is valid."],"key":"Do not answer \\"no power because it is not listed.\\" Residual power is the answer, but it still needs a factual basis."},{"id":"risos","part":1,"n":2,"name":"Risos-Vidal v. COMELEC and Lim","cite":"G.R. No. 206666 · Jan. 21, 2015","hook":"“Whereas” is not a condition.","rule":"Pardon power is plenary; a pardon restoring \\"civil and political rights\\" lets the person run, and a \\"whereas\\" clause is not a condition.","mem":["“…the President may grant reprieves, commutations, and pardons, and remit fines and forfeitures, after conviction by final judgment.” — Art. VII, Sec. 19","“No pardon… for violation of election laws… shall be granted by the President without the favorable recommendation of the Commission.” — Art. IX-C, Sec. 5"],"doc":["The pardoning power is **plenary**. Congress cannot add limits by law.","A condition binds only if it is in the **operative (dispositive) part**, not in a \\"whereas\\" clause."],"drill":"A former governor is convicted with finality of graft, with perpetual disqualification. The President pardons him and \\"restores his civil and political rights.\\" The preamble notes he \\"intends to retire from politics.\\" He later runs for Congress. A rival seeks his disqualification. Will it prosper?","alac":["No. He may run.","The Constitution gives the President the power to pardon after conviction by final judgment, except in impeachment cases. Jurisprudence provides that this power is plenary. A pardon restoring \\"civil and political rights\\" includes the right to run. A whereas clause is not a condition unless the operative part says so.","Here, the conviction was final. The pardon restored his civil and political rights, which include the right to run. The retirement statement is only in the preamble, not in the operative part.","Therefore, he is not disqualified, and the petition must be denied."],"key":"Look WHERE the \\"condition\\" is written. Also check if the crime is an election offense — then COMELEC's recommendation is required."},{"id":"trillanes","part":1,"n":3,"name":"Trillanes v. Medialdea","cite":"G.R. No. 241494 · April 3, 2024","hook":"Two keys lock it. One key can't open it.","rule":"Amnesty needs the President plus a majority of all Members of Congress, so the President alone cannot revoke it; revoking it without a hearing also violates due process.","mem":["“He shall also have the power to grant amnesty with the concurrence of a majority of all the Members of the Congress.” — Art. VII, Sec. 19"],"doc":["Amnesty is a **joint act** of the President and Congress. The President **cannot revoke it alone**.","Revoking a granted amnesty without notice and hearing violates **due process**.","Delegating the **processing** of applications to the DND is valid (not undue delegation)."],"drill":"A President grants amnesty to rebels who surrender, and Congress concurs by a majority of all its Members. A DND committee approves Rebel R's application. Four years later, a new President cancels R's amnesty without asking R to explain, and orders his arrest. Is the order valid?","alac":["No. The order is not valid.","The Constitution provides that the President may grant amnesty only with the concurrence of a majority of all Members of Congress. Jurisprudence provides that the President cannot revoke it alone. The Constitution also provides that no person shall be deprived of liberty without due process.","Here, the amnesty had Congress's concurrence, and the DND committee validly approved R's application. The new President cancelled it alone. R was never given a chance to explain.","Therefore, the cancellation is void, and R keeps his amnesty."],"key":"Amnesty = majority of ALL Members of Congress. Treaty = 2/3 of ALL Senators. Don't mix them up."},{"id":"pangilinan","part":1,"n":4,"name":"Pangilinan v. Cayetano","cite":"G.R. No. 238875 · March 16, 2021","hook":"The exit door has two locks.","rule":"The President may withdraw from a treaty, except when the Senate made its concurrence depend on its consent to withdrawal, or when a later law implements the treaty.","mem":["“No treaty or international agreement shall be valid and effective unless concurred in by at least two-thirds of all the Members of the Senate.” — Art. VII, Sec. 21"],"doc":["The President, as primary architect of foreign policy, may withdraw — but this power is **not absolute**.","The President **may** withdraw from a treaty obviously contrary to the Constitution or a statute.","Senators suing individually lack standing if the **Senate as a body** passed no resolution."],"drill":"The Senate concurs in a treaty with this condition: \\"The Philippines shall not withdraw without the Senate's prior concurrence.\\" Years later, the President withdraws without asking the Senate. The Senate, by resolution, sues. Is the withdrawal valid?","alac":["No. The withdrawal is not valid.","The Constitution requires two-thirds of all Senators to concur in a treaty. Jurisprudence provides that the President may generally withdraw, but not when the Senate's concurrence was expressly conditioned on its consent to withdrawal.","Here, the Senate's resolution required its prior concurrence before any withdrawal. The President ignored this condition. The Senate sued as a body, so it has standing.","Therefore, the unilateral withdrawal is invalid."],"key":"Watch for standing (Senate as a body) and mootness (withdrawal already effective). Mention mootness exceptions if needed."},{"id":"colmenares","part":1,"n":5,"name":"Colmenares v. Duterte","cite":"G.R. Nos. 245981 & 246594 · Aug. 9, 2022","hook":"Can't sue the President. Loan needs the Monetary Board.","rule":"A sitting President cannot be sued in any case; foreign loans need the Monetary Board's concurrence; information on foreign loans must be made public.","mem":["“The President may contract or guarantee foreign loans… with the prior concurrence of the Monetary Board.” — Art. VII, Sec. 20","“Information on foreign loans obtained or guaranteed by the Government shall be made available to the public.” — Art. XII, Sec. 21"],"doc":["**Presidential immunity:** a sitting President cannot be sued, whatever the case. Drop the President; the case continues against the other officials.","Loans are valid if made with **Monetary Board concurrence**.","**Confidentiality clauses** cannot defeat the right to information on foreign loans."],"drill":"Citizens file prohibition against \\"the President and the Finance Secretary\\" to stop a foreign loan, claiming (1) no Monetary Board approval and (2) a secrecy clause. (a) Should the President be dropped? (b) If the MB never concurred, is the loan valid?","alac":["(a) Yes, drop the President. (b) No, the loan is invalid.","Jurisprudence provides that a sitting President is immune from suit regardless of the type of case. The Constitution provides that foreign loans need the prior concurrence of the Monetary Board, and that information on foreign loans shall be public.","(a) Here, the sitting President is named, so he must be dropped; the case continues against the Secretary. (b) The MB never concurred, which is required. The secrecy clause also cannot block public access.","Therefore, the President is dropped, and the loan is invalid."],"key":"Immunity is personal and only during the term — the case goes on against the alter egos. MB concurrence ≠ Senate concurrence."},{"id":"garafil","part":1,"n":6,"name":"Velicaria-Garafil v. Office of the President","cite":"G.R. No. 203372 · June 16, 2015","hook":"Appointment is a four-step dance. Finish before the music stops.","rule":"Appointment is a process; all four steps must be finished before the two-month election ban starts.","mem":["“Two months immediately before the next presidential elections and up to the end of his term, a President or Acting President shall not make appointments, except temporary appointments to executive positions when continued vacancies therein will prejudice public service or endanger public safety.” — Art. VII, Sec. 15"],"doc":[],"drill":"The election is on May 8. On March 6, the outgoing President signs Atty. P's appointment as Assistant Secretary. It is released on March 12, and P takes her oath on March 15. The new President removes her. P says she was appointed on March 6. Who is correct?","alac":["The new President is correct. P's appointment is void.","The Constitution bans appointments two months before the presidential election, except temporary appointments to executive positions when vacancies will prejudice public service or endanger public safety. Jurisprudence provides that appointment is a process — signing, transmittal, vacancy, and acceptance — that must be completed before the ban.","Here, the ban began March 8. Only the signing (March 6) came before it. Transmittal (March 12) and acceptance (March 15) happened during the ban. The appointment was permanent, so the exception does not apply.","Therefore, P's appointment is a void midnight appointment."],"key":"Count two months back from ELECTION DAY, not from the end of the term."},{"id":"decastro","part":1,"n":7,"name":"De Castro v. JBC","cite":"G.R. No. 191002 · March 17, 2010","hook":"The ban is for the Executive, not the judges.","rule":"The appointments ban covers only the Executive Department, so the President may fill Judiciary vacancies (even Chief Justice) during the ban.","mem":["“Any vacancy shall be filled within ninety days from the occurrence thereof.” — Art. VIII, Sec. 4(1)"],"doc":["Sec. 15 is in **Article VII** (Executive). The framers did not put it in Article VIII.","Art. VIII, Sec. 4(1) says \\"shall\\" — the **90-day period is mandatory**.","The **JBC process** already prevents the abuses the ban targets.","*In re Valenzuela* (1998) was **reversed**."],"drill":"An SC Justice retires April 2 of an election year. The election is May 11. The JBC submits a list April 20. On April 25, the President appoints Justice J. A lawyer says this violates the ban. Decide.","alac":["The appointment is valid.","The Constitution bans appointments two months before the election, but it also requires SC vacancies to be filled within 90 days. Jurisprudence provides that the ban applies only to the Executive Department, not the Judiciary.","Here, the vacancy arose April 2 and was filled April 25, within 90 days. Even if April 25 is in the ban period, the ban does not cover the SC. J came from the JBC list.","Therefore, the appointment is valid."],"key":"SC: 90 days from the VACANCY. Lower courts: 90 days from the JBC LIST."},{"id":"belgica","part":2,"n":8,"name":"Belgica v. Executive Secretary","cite":"G.R. No. 208566 · Nov. 19, 2013","hook":"After the budget passes, hands off.","rule":"Legislators cannot control funds after the budget is passed; and \\"such other purposes as the President may direct\\" is undue delegation.","mem":["“No money shall be paid out of the Treasury except in pursuance of an appropriation made by law.” — Art. VI, Sec. 29(1)","“The President shall have the power to veto any particular item or items in an appropriation… bill.” — Art. VI, Sec. 27(2)"],"doc":["**Item** = specific amount for a specific purpose (\\"singular correspondence\\").","**Delegation tests:** completeness test + sufficient standard test."],"drill":"The GAA has a P20-billion \\"Local Infrastructure Program.\\" A provision lets each House Member list district projects for DPWH to implement and move funds between projects. No projects are named. The President signed without veto. (a) Is it constitutional? (b) Does the signature cure it?","alac":["(a) No. (b) No.","The Constitution vests legislative power in Congress as a body and gives the President item veto. Jurisprudence provides that after the budget is enacted, legislators cannot identify projects, realign, or release funds, because this violates separation of powers, non-delegability, and checks and balances.","(a) Here, Members choose projects and move funds after enactment — executive work. Each Member decides how money is spent. The lump sum leaves no items to veto. (b) A signature cannot cure what the Constitution prohibits.","Therefore, the provision is void."],"key":"PRE-enactment proposals are allowed. POST-enactment control is banned."},{"id":"sereno","part":3,"n":9,"name":"Republic v. Sereno","cite":"G.R. No. 237428 · May 11, 2018","hook":"Not qualified from the start → quo warranto. Bad acts in office → impeachment.","rule":"Quo warranto (filed in the Supreme Court) can oust an impeachable officer who was not eligible when appointed; the one-year limit does not bind the State.","mem":["“A Member of the Judiciary must be a person of proven competence, integrity, probity, and independence.” — Art. VIII, Sec. 7(3)","“…may be removed from office on impeachment…” — Art. XI, Sec. 2"],"doc":["\\"**May**\\" in Art. XI, Sec. 2 is permissive — impeachment is not the only way.","One-year prescription does **not** run against the State.","Failure to file SALNs = lack of proven **integrity**."],"drill":"An SC Justice appointed in 2020 is found in 2027 to have been naturalized abroad without reacquiring citizenship when she applied. The SolGen files quo warranto. She says (1) only impeachment can remove her, and (2) it was filed too late. Rule.","alac":["Deny the motion to dismiss.","The Constitution provides that impeachable officers \\"may\\" be removed by impeachment and gives the SC jurisdiction over quo warranto. Jurisprudence provides that quo warranto may question eligibility at appointment, and the one-year period does not run against the State. SC Members must be natural-born citizens.","(1) Here, the issue is her eligibility at appointment, not an offense in office, so quo warranto fits. (2) The State filed through the SolGen, so the period does not apply.","Therefore, the petition is proper and timely."],"key":"Eligibility problem → quo warranto. Misconduct after appointment → impeachment."},{"id":"jardeleza","part":3,"n":10,"name":"Jardeleza v. Sereno","cite":"G.R. No. 213181 · Aug. 19, 2014","hook":"Strategy is not integrity. A charge must be heard.","rule":"When the JBC questions an applicant's integrity, it must give notice and a hearing; a disagreement about legal strategy is not an integrity issue.","mem":["“A Judicial and Bar Council is hereby created under the supervision of the Supreme Court…” — Art. VIII, Sec. 8(1)"],"doc":["JBC proceedings are *sui generis*, but **due process applies** when integrity is challenged.","The unanimity rule applies only to **integrity** questions, not legal strategy.","The SC corrects the JBC through its **supervision** and its power over grave abuse."],"drill":"A JBC member says a CA Justice \\"took a weak legal position in a famous case — an integrity issue.\\" Using the unanimity rule, the JBC excludes her. She learns of it only after the list comes out. Was this proper?","alac":["No.","The Constitution places the JBC under the supervision of the Supreme Court. Jurisprudence provides that due process applies when an applicant's integrity is challenged, and that legal strategy is not a matter of integrity.","Here, the objection is about legal judgment, not honesty, so the unanimity rule should not apply. She was also never told or heard before exclusion.","Therefore, the JBC committed grave abuse of discretion."],"key":"Two errors: wrong label (strategy ≠ integrity) AND no notice/hearing. Mention both."},{"id":"villanueva","part":3,"n":11,"name":"Villanueva v. JBC","cite":"G.R. No. 211833 · April 7, 2015","hook":"JBC may add rules — but must publish them.","rule":"The JBC may add reasonable standards beyond the minimum qualifications, but it must publish them.","mem":["“The Council shall have the principal function of recommending appointees to the Judiciary.” — Art. VIII, Sec. 8(5)"],"doc":["The JBC may set **additional standards** to measure \\"proven competence.\\"","The 5-year rule for first-level judges is a **reasonable classification** (experience) — no equal protection violation.","Applicants have **no vested right** to be nominated.","Policies must be **published**."],"drill":"The JBC adopts an internal, unpublished rule: only lawyers with 10 years in government may apply to the CA. A private lawyer with 20 years' practice is excluded. He argues (1) the JBC cannot add qualifications and (2) the rule was not published. Decide.","alac":["Wrong on (1), correct on (2).","Jurisprudence provides that the JBC may adopt reasonable standards beyond the minimum, but such policies must be published.","(1) Here, adding criteria is within the JBC's power, though favoring only government lawyers may be unreasonable. (2) The rule was never published, so applicants had no notice.","Therefore, the JBC cannot enforce this unpublished rule against him."],"key":"\\"JBC cannot add rules\\" is a trap. Attack reasonableness and publication instead."},{"id":"chavez","part":3,"n":12,"name":"Chavez v. JBC","cite":"G.R. No. 202242 · July 17, 2012","hook":"\\"A\\" representative means ONE.","rule":"Only one representative of Congress sits in the seven-member JBC; past JBC acts remain valid (operative fact).","mem":["“…the Chief Justice as ex officio Chairman, the Secretary of Justice, and a representative of the Congress as ex officio Members, a representative of the Integrated Bar, a professor of law, a retired Member of the Supreme Court, and a representative of the private sector.” — Art. VIII, Sec. 8(1)"],"doc":["**Plain meaning** (verba legis): \\"a representative\\" = one.","Seven members is deliberate — an odd number avoids deadlock.","**Operative fact doctrine:** past acts stay valid."],"drill":"A law gives the Senate and House each one JBC representative with a half vote each. Valid?","alac":["No.","The Constitution fixes the JBC at seven members, including \\"a representative of the Congress.\\" Jurisprudence provides this means only one.","Here, the law creates two representatives, making eight members. Half votes do not cure the extra seat. Congress cannot amend the Constitution by statute.","Therefore, the law is unconstitutional."],"key":"Splitting the vote does not fix the extra seat."},{"id":"aguinaldo","part":3,"n":13,"name":"Aguinaldo v. Aquino","cite":"G.R. No. 224302 · Nov. 29, 2016","hook":"No clusters. The President picks from the whole basket.","rule":"For vacancies that exist at the same time, the JBC cannot cluster nominees; the President may choose from all nominees.","mem":["“…shall be appointed by the President from a list of at least three nominees prepared by the Judicial and Bar Council for every vacancy.” — Art. VIII, Sec. 9"],"doc":["**Clustering** limits the President's choice — it encroaches on the appointing power.","\\"Three per vacancy\\" is met if the combined list has at least 3 × the number of vacancies.","Seniority follows the **order of appointment**."],"drill":"Three CA seats open the same day. JBC sends three lists (4, 3, 3 names). The President picks two from List A and one from List C. A List B nominee sues. Decide.","alac":["The appointments are valid.","The Constitution provides that the President appoints from at least three JBC nominees for every vacancy. Jurisprudence provides that the JBC cannot cluster nominees for simultaneous vacancies.","Here, the vacancies arose together, so they form one group. Ten nominees for three seats exceeds three per vacancy. The JBC's clusters cannot bind the President.","Therefore, the appointments are valid."],"key":"The JBC RECOMMENDS; the President APPOINTS."},{"id":"recoa","part":3,"n":14,"name":"Re: COA Opinion on Appraised Value of SC Properties","cite":"A.M. No. 11-7-10-SC · July 31, 2012","hook":"The Court sells its own things its own way.","rule":"COA may audit the Judiciary, but it cannot force its own valuation formula on how the Court disposes of its own property.","mem":["“The Judiciary shall enjoy fiscal autonomy.” — Art. VIII, Sec. 3"],"doc":["**Fiscal autonomy** = full flexibility to allocate and use resources, including disposing of property under the Court's own guidelines.","COA still **audits** — it just cannot dictate the Court's valuation."],"drill":"The SC lets retiring employees buy used computers at 10% of cost under its own guidelines. COA disallows and demands a higher price under its circular. Valid?","alac":["No.","The Constitution grants the Judiciary fiscal autonomy. Jurisprudence provides this covers disposing of its own property under its own guidelines; COA cannot impose its formula.","Here, the Court applied its own guidelines to its own property.","Therefore, the disallowance is invalid."],"key":"Fiscal autonomy ≠ exemption from audit."},{"id":"jdf","part":3,"n":15,"name":"In re: Judicial Independence v. Abolition of JDF","cite":"UDK-15143 · Jan. 21, 2015","hook":"A bill is not a law. No case yet.","rule":"Courts cannot review a proposed bill because there is no actual case yet.","mem":["“Appropriations for the Judiciary may not be reduced by the legislature below the amount appropriated for the previous year and, after approval, shall be automatically and regularly released.” — Art. VIII, Sec. 3"],"doc":["Mandamus cannot compel the Court to act against Congress on its own autonomy.","The JDF comes from **legal fees**, not national appropriations."],"drill":"A Senator files a bill cutting the Judiciary's budget 20% below this year. Before committee hearing, lawyers ask the SC to strike it down. Should the Court rule?","alac":["No.","The Constitution provides that judicial power settles actual controversies. Jurisprudence requires an actual case; a proposed bill is not ripe.","Here, the cut exists only in a bill not yet heard. It may change or die. No one is injured yet.","Therefore, the petition should be dismissed."],"key":"Strong fiscal-autonomy argument + no actual case = still dismissed. Check the requisites FIRST."},{"id":"castaneda","part":3,"n":16,"name":"OCA / Flores-Concepcion v. Judge Castañeda","cite":"A.M. No. RTJ-15-2438 · Sept. 2, 2020","hook":"Retire: the case lives. Die: the case dies.","rule":"Retirement or resignation does not end an administrative case filed while in office; death generally does.","mem":["“The Supreme Court shall have administrative supervision over all courts and the personnel thereof.” — Art. VIII, Sec. 6"],"doc":["**Rule:** Jurisdiction attaches when the complaint is filed while the respondent is in office; leaving office does not end it."],"drill":"A complaint is filed against Judge J in 2025. He retires in 2026. The OCA recommends a fine. (a) Did the Court lose jurisdiction? (b) What if he had died instead?","alac":["(a) No. (b) Generally, yes, the case would be dismissed.","The Constitution gives the SC administrative supervision over courts. Jurisprudence provides that retirement does not end a case filed while in office, but death generally does, unless due process was violated, there are equitable reasons, or the penalty makes it meaningful.","(a) Here, the complaint was filed while J was in office; the fine can come from retirement benefits. (b) If he died, he could not defend himself, and a fine would burden his heirs.","Therefore, jurisdiction stays despite retirement, but death would generally lead to dismissal."],"key":"Retirement ≠ death. Note the three exceptions to the death rule."},{"id":"caronan","part":3,"n":17,"name":"Caronan v. Caronan","cite":"A.C. No. 11316 · July 12, 2016","hook":"Fake identity → no lawyer.","rule":"The practice of law is a privilege; admission obtained by fraud is void.","mem":["“Promulgate rules concerning… admission to the practice of law…” — Art. VIII, Sec. 5(5)"],"doc":["Law practice is a **privilege**, not a right.","Education and **moral fitness** must exist at admission and **continue**.","Years of honest practice do not cure fraudulent admission."],"drill":"Atty. M passed the bar in 2019. In 2026, it is found she used forged college records. She says (1) she practiced honestly for 7 years and (2) her admission is final. Decide.","alac":["Her arguments fail.","The Constitution gives the SC power over admission to the practice of law. Jurisprudence provides that it is a privilege requiring continuous qualifications, and fraudulent admission is invalid.","Here, she never met the education requirement and hid it with forgery. Honest practice cannot cure fraud, and a privilege can be revisited anytime.","Therefore, her name may be stricken and she may be barred."],"key":"Congress cannot \\"admit\\" or \\"reinstate\\" lawyers by law — this power is the Court's."},{"id":"brillantes","part":4,"n":18,"name":"Brillantes v. Yorac","cite":"G.R. No. 93867 · Dec. 18, 1990","hook":"No acting chair from the Palace.","rule":"The President cannot designate an acting chairman of a Constitutional Commission, even temporarily.","mem":["“The Constitutional Commissions, which shall be independent, are the Civil Service Commission, the Commission on Elections, and the Commission on Audit.” — Art. IX-A, Sec. 1","“In no case shall any Member be appointed or designated in a temporary or acting capacity.” — Art. IX-C, Sec. 1(2) / IX-B / IX-D"],"doc":["A designation revocable at will makes the officer **depend on the President** — this kills independence.","A temporary arrangement is for the **Commission itself** to decide."],"drill":"The CSC Chair takes a 6-month leave. The President designates Commissioner C as \\"OIC and Acting Chairperson.\\" Valid?","alac":["No.","The Constitution makes the Commissions independent and prohibits any temporary or acting designation. Jurisprudence provides the President cannot designate an acting chairman.","Here, C would serve at the President's pleasure, which undermines the CSC's independence.","Therefore, the designation is void."],"key":"\\"Temporary\\" or \\"OIC\\" labels don't save it."},{"id":"funa","part":4,"n":19,"name":"Funa v. Villar (Chairman, COA)","cite":"G.R. No. 192791 · April 24, 2012","hook":"Why is the seat empty? Then add up the years — never over 7.","rule":"Expired term → full 7 years; death/resignation/disability/impeachment → only the leftover term; total service never over 7.","mem":["“…for a term of seven years without reappointment… Appointment to any vacancy shall be only for the unexpired portion of the term of the predecessor. In no case shall any Member be appointed or designated in a temporary or acting capacity.” — Art. IX-D, Sec. 1(2)"],"doc":["**Expired** term → next appointment is a **full 7 years**; less is void.","**Death, resignation, disability, impeachment** → only the **unexpired portion**.","Served a full 7 years → **never** reappointed.","No temporary or acting appointment."],"drill":"Commissioner K has served 3 years. The Chairman resigns with 4 years left. The President appoints K as Chairman \\"for 7 years.\\" (a) Valid? (b) What appointment would be valid?","alac":["(a) No. (b) Chairman for the remaining 4 years.","The Constitution provides 7-year terms without reappointment, and appointment to a vacancy only for the unexpired portion. Jurisprudence allows a Commissioner to become Chairman when the vacancy is from resignation, for the unexpired portion only, with total service not over 7.","(a) Here, the vacancy is from resignation, so only 4 years remain. Seven years would also total 10. (b) Four years as Chairman gives a total of exactly 7.","Therefore, the 7-year appointment is void, but a 4-year appointment is valid."],"key":"First ask WHY the seat is vacant. Then add the years."},{"id":"pililla","part":4,"n":20,"name":"CSC v. Pililla Water District","cite":"G.R. No. 190147 · March 5, 2013","hook":"Confidence lost = term over (not removal).","rule":"A water district GM holds a primarily confidential position; courts decide this by the job's nature; loss of confidence ends the term and is not removal.","mem":["“Appointments in the civil service shall be made only according to merit and fitness… except to positions which are policy-determining, primarily confidential, or highly technical…” — Art. IX-B, Sec. 2(2)"],"doc":["**Proximity rule:** look at the job's actual functions and closeness to the appointing power.","**Courts**, not the CSC, have the final say.","Loss of confidence = **expiration of term**, not removal."],"drill":"A water district Board declares loss of confidence in its GM and replaces him. He says he can only be removed for cause, citing a CSC circular calling GM career service. Correct?","alac":["No.","The Constitution exempts primarily confidential positions from merit-and-fitness rules. Jurisprudence provides that a water district GM is primarily confidential, courts decide this, and the term ends on loss of confidence.","Here, the GM works closely with and at the pleasure of the Board. The circular does not bind courts. His term expired; he was not removed.","Therefore, the Board validly replaced him."],"key":"Water districts are GOCCs with original charters — covered by CSC and audited by COA."},{"id":"ppi","part":4,"n":21,"name":"Philippine Press Institute v. COMELEC","cite":"G.R. No. 119694 · May 22, 1995","hook":"Free newspaper space = taking. Free airtime = OK.","rule":"COMELEC cannot force newspapers to give free space (taking without just compensation), but broadcasters may be required to give free airtime.","mem":["“Private property shall not be taken for public use without just compensation.” — Art. III, Sec. 9","COMELEC may “supervise or regulate the enjoyment or utilization of all franchises or permits…” for media during the election period — Art. IX-C, Sec. 4"],"doc":["Forced donation of print space is **eminent domain without pay**.","Not valid police power — **no necessity** shown.","\\"Supervise or regulate\\" does **not** include a power to take."],"drill":"COMELEC orders (1) newspapers to publish a free full page of platforms weekly and (2) TV networks to give 30 free minutes daily. Valid?","alac":["(1) Invalid. (2) Valid.","The Constitution prohibits taking private property without just compensation and lets COMELEC regulate media franchises. Jurisprudence holds forced free print space is a taking, while broadcast franchises may carry free airtime conditions.","(1) Here, newspapers need no franchise; forcing free pages takes their property. (2) TV networks use airwaves under a franchise, a privilege.","Therefore, rule (1) is void and rule (2) is valid."],"key":"Print vs. broadcast is the classic trap."},{"id":"penera","part":4,"n":22,"name":"Peñera v. COMELEC","cite":"G.R. No. 181613 · Nov. 25, 2009 (MR)","hook":"Candidate only when the campaign starts.","rule":"A person who files a COC becomes a candidate only at the start of the campaign period, so acts before it are not premature campaigning.","mem":["“Any person who files his certificate of candidacy… shall only be considered as a candidate at the start of the campaign period…” — R.A. 8436, Sec. 15, as amended by R.A. 9369"],"doc":["Unlawful acts that apply to **candidates** take effect only at the start of the campaign period.","The Court **reversed** its Sept. 11, 2009 decision on reconsideration (Nov. 25, 2009)."],"drill":"M files her COC on Oct. 1. The campaign period starts March 28. In December, she hands out \\"M for Mayor\\" shirts and holds a rally. A rival seeks disqualification. Will it prosper?","alac":["No.","The law provides that one who files a COC becomes a candidate only at the start of the campaign period. Jurisprudence applies this to hold there is no premature campaigning before then.","Here, M's acts were in December, before March 28. She was not yet legally a candidate.","Therefore, she cannot be disqualified for premature campaigning."],"key":"Cite the November 2009 Resolution. Other laws (vote-buying, misuse of funds) still apply."},{"id":"seneres","part":4,"n":23,"name":"Señeres v. COMELEC","cite":"G.R. No. 178678 · April 16, 2009","hook":"Oath taken → go to the HRET.","rule":"Once party-list nominees are proclaimed, sworn in, and assume office, only the HRET can hear challenges to them.","mem":["“The Senate and the House of Representatives shall each have an Electoral Tribunal which shall be the sole judge of all contests relating to the election, returns, and qualifications of their respective Members.” — Art. VI, Sec. 17"],"doc":["**Hold-over:** an officer whose term expired may continue until a successor is elected, unless party rules forbid.","**Estoppel:** one who benefited from an officer's acts cannot later question that officer's authority."],"drill":"Party-list P's nominees are proclaimed, sworn in, and assume office in July. In August, a member asks COMELEC to cancel N1's nomination. Does COMELEC have jurisdiction?","alac":["No.","The Constitution makes the HRET the sole judge of contests on qualifications of House Members. Jurisprudence provides that after proclamation, oath, and assumption, jurisdiction passes to the HRET.","Here, N1 completed all three in July, before the August petition.","Therefore, COMELEC must dismiss; the remedy is with the HRET."],"key":"All three acts needed. Party-list nominees are House Members too."},{"id":"lp","part":4,"n":24,"name":"Liberal Party v. COMELEC","cite":"G.R. No. 191771 · May 6, 2010","hook":"Register first, accredit after. A deadline is a deadline.","rule":"A coalition must register before it can be accredited, and registration deadlines are mandatory, including for coalitions.","mem":["COMELEC shall “register, after sufficient publication, political parties, organizations, or coalitions…” — Art. IX-C, Sec. 2(5)"],"doc":["Registration is **not** mere recognition of an \\"operative fact\\"; it requires discretion and quasi-judicial evaluation.","Granting **late** registration is grave abuse of discretion."],"drill":"Parties A and B form Coalition AB three weeks after the deadline. COMELEC registers and accredits AB, saying the deadline covers only new parties and AB is an \\"operative fact.\\" Correct?","alac":["No.","The Constitution empowers COMELEC to register parties and coalitions. Jurisprudence provides that registration deadlines are mandatory for coalitions, registration is required, and accreditation comes only after registration.","Here, AB applied late. It cannot be treated as an operative fact. Without valid registration, it cannot be accredited.","Therefore, both are void for grave abuse."],"key":"The Court left open whether COMELEC en banc may act on registration in the first instance."},{"id":"galindo","part":4,"n":25,"name":"Galindo v. COA","cite":"G.R. No. 210788 · Jan. 10, 2017","hook":"COA as boss → appeal to CSC. Auditors take nothing from auditees.","rule":"When COA disciplines its own staff, the remedy is appeal to the CSC, not certiorari to the SC; auditors may not take benefits from the agencies they audit.","mem":["“…any decision, order, or ruling of each Commission may be brought to the Supreme Court on certiorari… within thirty days…” — Art. IX-A, Sec. 7"],"doc":["Substantial evidence is enough in administrative cases."],"drill":"A COA auditor accepts a monthly meal allowance from the university he audits. COA suspends him 6 months. He files certiorari with the SC. (a) Right remedy? (b) Offense?","alac":["(a) No. (b) Yes.","The Constitution allows certiorari to the SC from Commission decisions. Jurisprudence provides that COA's discipline of its own personnel follows civil service rules and is appealable to the CSC, and COA staff cannot take benefits from audited agencies.","(a) Here, a 6-month suspension of its own auditor is discipline as employer, so appeal to the CSC. (b) Taking an allowance from the auditee compromises independence.","Therefore, dismiss the petition; the auditor is liable."],"key":"Certiorari (IX-A, Sec. 7) is for COA's constitutional functions, not personnel discipline."},{"id":"bsp","part":4,"n":26,"name":"BSP v. COA","cite":"G.R. No. 210314 · Oct. 12, 2021","hook":"No stock, no members → not a GOCC.","rule":"The BSP is not a GOCC (no stock, no members), so GOCC laws do not apply to it, but COA still audits it.","mem":["“The Congress shall establish an independent central monetary authority…” — Art. XII, Sec. 20","“No law shall be passed exempting any entity of the Government… from the jurisdiction of the Commission on Audit.” — Art. IX-D, Sec. 3"],"doc":["BSP = **instrumentality with corporate powers**, not stock or non-stock.","COA may decide questions of law in an audit, but must apply the **correct law**."],"drill":"COA disallows an instrumentality's deduction of reserves, applying a law for \\"GOCCs.\\" The entity has no capital stock or members. Correct?","alac":["No.","The Constitution lets COA audit all agencies, GOCCs, and instrumentalities. Jurisprudence provides that an entity with no stock and no members is an instrumentality, not a GOCC.","Here, the entity is an instrumentality. COA may audit it but applied a law covering only GOCCs.","Therefore, the disallowance should be set aside."],"key":"Not a GOCC does NOT mean not audited."},{"id":"miaa","part":4,"n":27,"name":"MIAA v. City of Pasay","cite":"G.R. No. 163072 · April 2, 2009","hook":"Airport is public. The leased part pays tax.","rule":"MIAA is a government instrumentality; airport property is exempt from real property tax, except parts leased to private persons.","mem":["“Each local government unit shall have the power to create its own sources of revenues and to levy taxes… subject to such guidelines and limitations as the Congress may provide…” — Art. X, Sec. 5"],"doc":["Airport lands and buildings are **property of public dominion** owned by the Republic."],"drill":"City X taxes all airport-authority land: (1) runways and terminals; (2) a portion leased to a private hotel. The authority has no stock or members. What may be taxed?","alac":["Only the leased hotel portion.","The Constitution lets LGUs tax subject to limits set by Congress. The law exempts Republic-owned property unless its use is given to a taxable person. Jurisprudence holds an airport authority with no stock and members is an instrumentality, and airport property is public dominion.","Here, runways and terminals are public dominion, exempt. The hotel portion was leased to a private company.","Therefore, City X may tax only the leased portion."],"key":"Pair with BSP v. COA — same no-stock, no-members test."},{"id":"chrea","part":4,"n":28,"name":"CHREA v. CHR","cite":"G.R. No. 155336 · Nov. 25, 2004","hook":"Only J-C-O have fiscal autonomy.","rule":"Only the Judiciary, the Constitutional Commissions, and the Ombudsman (J-C-O) have fiscal autonomy; the CHR does not, so it needs DBM approval.","mem":["Fiscal autonomy appears only in: Art. VIII, Sec. 3 (Judiciary) · Art. IX-A, Sec. 5 (Commissions) · Art. XI, Sec. 14 (Ombudsman)"],"doc":["**Independent ≠ fiscally autonomous.**","Upgrading or reclassifying positions needs **DBM approval** under the Salary Standardization Law."],"drill":"An \\"independent\\" body under the social justice article raises its division chiefs' salary grades without DBM approval. Valid?","alac":["No.","The Constitution grants fiscal autonomy only to the Judiciary, the Commissions, and the Ombudsman. Jurisprudence provides that offices like the CHR need DBM approval.","Here, the body is none of the three. Independence is not fiscal autonomy.","Therefore, the upgrade is invalid."],"key":"Memorize J-C-O."}]`);
const TW = /* @__PURE__ */ JSON.parse(`[{"c":"marcos","t":"same","label":"Fact-finding commission","facts":"A newly built government bridge collapses, killing 40 people. The President issues an executive order creating a five-member “Bridge Collapse Commission” to investigate and recommend charges to the DOJ. No law authorizes the commission. The contractors argue that only Congress can create public offices, so the order is void.","q":"Is the executive order valid?","hint":"Is the commission deciding cases, or only finding facts and recommending?","alac":["Yes. The executive order creating the commission is valid.","The Constitution vests the executive power in the President and requires the President to ensure that the laws are faithfully executed. Jurisprudence provides that executive power is broader than the powers listed, and that the President may create ad hoc fact-finding bodies to gather facts and recommend action, as long as these bodies do not decide cases or exercise powers that belong to Congress or the courts.","Here, the commission only investigates the collapse and recommends charges to the DOJ. It does not try anyone or impose penalties. This means it is a tool the President uses to see that the laws on public works and safety are carried out, not a new office with powers of its own.","Therefore, the contractors are wrong. The executive order is valid."],"key":"A fact-finding body that only investigates and recommends is within executive power. If it starts deciding cases or punishing people, it crosses into judicial power."},{"c":"marcos","t":"same","label":"State of national emergency","facts":"After a series of bombings in three cities, the President issues a proclamation declaring a “state of national emergency” and orders the AFP and PNP to suppress lawless violence. No law requires or authorizes such a declaration. Opposition legislators argue that the President cannot declare something the Constitution does not mention.","q":"Is the proclamation valid?","hint":"Does the declaration give the President any new power, or only describe the situation?","alac":["Yes. The proclamation is valid.","The Constitution vests the executive power in the President and makes the President the Commander-in-Chief, who may call out the armed forces to suppress lawless violence. Jurisprudence provides that the President's powers are not limited to those listed, and that a declaration of a state of emergency or rebellion is valid when it only describes the situation and adds no new powers.","Here, the bombings show real lawless violence, so there is a factual basis. The proclamation only describes the situation and directs the AFP and PNP to act, which the President may already do as Commander-in-Chief. It does not suspend any right or take over any private business.","Therefore, the proclamation is valid."],"key":"A declaration that only describes a crisis is valid. But the President cannot use it to take over private businesses or suspend rights; that needs a law or the specific steps the Constitution requires."},{"c":"marcos","t":"twist","label":"National ID by executive order","facts":"Citing “residual powers to protect the general welfare,” the President issues an administrative order creating a national ID system. Every citizen must submit fingerprints and iris scans, and government offices must refuse service to anyone without the ID. Agencies are told to use their existing budgets. No law authorizes the system.","q":"Is the administrative order valid under the President's residual powers?","hint":"Residual power fills gaps in executive action. Can it be used to make a law?","alac":["No. The administrative order is invalid.","The Constitution vests legislative power in Congress and executive power in the President. Jurisprudence provides that the President has residual powers, but these cannot be used to make law. A measure that creates new duties for citizens, affects their rights, and needs public funds must come from Congress.","Here, the order requires every citizen to give biometric data and cuts off government services to those who refuse. It also spends public money with no law appropriating it. These are matters only a law can cover, so this is lawmaking, not executing the law.","Therefore, residual power does not save the order. It is invalid for usurping legislative power."],"key":"TWIST: residual power is not a blank check. Ask: is the President executing or protecting, or is the President making new rules that bind citizens? The second needs a law."},{"c":"risos","t":"same","label":"Congress limits pardon","facts":"Congress passes a law stating that “no person pardoned for plunder may run for public office within ten years from the pardon.” A year later, the President pardons former Mayor M, who was convicted of plunder with perpetual disqualification, and expressly restores his civil and political rights. M files a COC for governor. A rival cites the new law.","q":"May M run for governor?","hint":"Who can put limits on the pardoning power?","alac":["Yes. M may run for governor.","The Constitution gives the President the power to grant pardons after conviction by final judgment, except in impeachment cases and, for election law violations, without COMELEC's favorable recommendation. Jurisprudence provides that the pardoning power is plenary, so Congress cannot add limits by law. A pardon that expressly restores civil and political rights includes the right to run.","Here, M was convicted with finality, the crime is not an election offense, and the case is not impeachment. The pardon expressly restored his civil and political rights. The ten-year waiting rule is a limit made by Congress, which the Constitution does not allow.","Therefore, the law cannot be applied to M's pardon, and he may run for governor."],"key":"The only limits on pardon are the ones the Constitution lists. A statute that adds more is void as applied to the pardon."},{"c":"risos","t":"same","label":"No Board recommendation","facts":"The President pardons convict C, who is serving time for homicide with a final conviction. The Board of Pardons and Parole never recommended it, and the victim's family opposed it publicly. The family files a petition arguing that the pardon is void because the Board did not recommend it.","q":"Is the pardon void?","hint":"Is a Board recommendation one of the constitutional limits?","alac":["No. The pardon is valid.","The Constitution gives the President the power to grant pardons after conviction by final judgment. It sets only three limits: there must be a final conviction, it cannot apply to impeachment, and election offenses need COMELEC's favorable recommendation. Jurisprudence provides that the pardoning power is plenary and that the President's choice cannot be reviewed for wisdom.","Here, C's conviction for homicide is final. The case is not impeachment, and homicide is not an election offense. A recommendation from the Board is not a constitutional requirement, and the family's opposition does not limit the President.","Therefore, the pardon is valid."],"key":"Run through the three limits only: final conviction, not impeachment, COMELEC for election offenses. Anything else is not a ground to void a pardon."},{"c":"risos","t":"twist","label":"Pardon while appeal is pending","facts":"Congressman K is convicted of estafa by the RTC and files an appeal with the Court of Appeals. While the appeal is pending, the President grants him an absolute pardon “restoring all his civil and political rights.” K does not withdraw his appeal, then files a COC for re-election. A rival asks COMELEC to disqualify him.","q":"Is the pardon valid?","hint":"Read the timing requirement in the Constitution.","alac":["No. The pardon is not valid while K's appeal is pending.","The Constitution provides that the President may grant pardons only after conviction by final judgment. Jurisprudence provides that a pardon given while an appeal is pending is void, unless the accused first withdraws the appeal so that the conviction becomes final.","Here, K appealed his conviction, and the appeal was still pending when the pardon was granted. He never withdrew it, so there was no conviction by final judgment yet. The words restoring his civil and political rights do not matter, because the pardon itself is premature.","Therefore, the pardon is not valid and does not help K."],"key":"TWIST: pardon is plenary, but timing is a constitutional limit. Check finality first. A pending appeal means no pardon yet, unless the appeal is withdrawn."},{"c":"trillanes","t":"same","label":"Amnesty without Congress","facts":"To end a long rebellion, the President issues a proclamation granting amnesty to all rebels who surrender within six months. The proclamation states it is “effective immediately” and was never submitted to Congress. Rebel R surrenders and asks the court to dismiss his rebellion case because of the amnesty.","q":"Should the case be dismissed because of the amnesty?","hint":"Who must act together to grant amnesty?","alac":["No. The amnesty is not yet effective, so the case should not be dismissed on that basis.","The Constitution provides that the President shall have the power to grant amnesty with the concurrence of a majority of all the Members of Congress. Jurisprudence provides that amnesty is a joint act of the President and Congress, so the President alone cannot grant or revoke it.","Here, the proclamation was never submitted to Congress, and Congress never concurred. The President acted alone. Without concurrence, the grant is incomplete, and R cannot claim its benefits.","Therefore, R's rebellion case should not be dismissed based on the proclamation."],"key":"Amnesty = President + majority of ALL Members of Congress. Missing either one means no amnesty."},{"c":"trillanes","t":"same","label":"Mass revocation without hearing","facts":"Five years after an amnesty with full congressional concurrence was granted to 300 soldiers, a new President issues a proclamation declaring all their amnesties “void from the start” because some applicants allegedly did not admit guilt. No soldier is asked to explain. The AFP is ordered to arrest them.","q":"Is the proclamation valid?","hint":"Two problems: who made the grant, and was anyone heard?","alac":["No. The proclamation is invalid.","The Constitution provides that amnesty is granted by the President with the concurrence of a majority of all the Members of Congress, and that no person shall be deprived of liberty without due process of law. Jurisprudence provides that the President alone cannot revoke an amnesty that was jointly granted, and that taking it away without notice and hearing violates due process.","Here, the amnesties were granted with Congress's concurrence, yet the new President revoked them alone. The soldiers were also never told of the claim that they failed to admit guilt or given a chance to answer, and they now face arrest.","Therefore, the proclamation is invalid, and the soldiers' amnesties stand."],"key":"Two separate grounds: (1) a joint act cannot be undone by one branch alone; (2) no notice and hearing means no due process. Use both in your answer."},{"c":"trillanes","t":"twist","label":"Conditional pardon revoked","facts":"The President grants convict P a conditional pardon on the condition that he “shall not again commit any crime.” A year later, P is charged with theft, but no court has convicted him yet. The President, after reviewing the police report, revokes the pardon and orders P back to prison. P argues that, like amnesty, the President cannot revoke alone and that he must first be convicted.","q":"Is the revocation valid?","hint":"Pardon and amnesty are granted differently. Who alone grants a pardon?","alac":["Yes. The revocation is valid.","The Constitution gives the President alone the power to grant pardons after conviction by final judgment, while amnesty needs Congress's concurrence. Jurisprudence provides that when a conditional pardon is violated, the President may determine the breach and order the convict recommitted, without waiting for a court conviction for the new offense.","Here, P received a conditional pardon, not an amnesty, so Congress had no part in it. The President found that P broke the condition based on the theft charge. Since the President alone granted the pardon, the President alone may revoke it when the condition is broken.","Therefore, P's argument fails, and the revocation is valid."],"key":"TWIST: amnesty (joint act) cannot be revoked by the President alone. A conditional pardon (President alone) can be, when its condition is broken. Name which act you have first."},{"c":"pangilinan","t":"same","label":"Withdrawal from a plain treaty","facts":"The Senate concurred in a regional fisheries treaty with no conditions. No law has been passed to implement it. The President notifies the other parties that the Philippines is withdrawing. The Senate passes a resolution objecting and files a petition, claiming that withdrawal also needs its concurrence.","q":"Is the withdrawal valid?","hint":"Check the two exceptions. Do any of them apply?","alac":["Yes. The withdrawal is valid.","The Constitution provides that no treaty shall be valid and effective unless concurred in by at least two-thirds of all the Members of the Senate. Jurisprudence provides that the President, as chief architect of foreign policy, may withdraw from a treaty, except when the Senate's concurrence was expressly conditioned on its consent to withdrawal, or when a later law affirms or implements the treaty.","Here, the Senate concurred without any condition on withdrawal, and no law implements the treaty. Neither exception applies. The Senate has standing because it acted as a body by resolution, but its claim fails on the merits.","Therefore, the President validly withdrew from the treaty."],"key":"Default: the President may withdraw. Then check the two exceptions: a Senate condition, or a later implementing law."},{"c":"pangilinan","t":"same","label":"Senators sue on their own","facts":"The President withdraws from a defense treaty. Three senators, acting on their own and without any Senate resolution, file a petition to stop the withdrawal. By the time the case is heard, the treaty's one-year notice period has passed, and the withdrawal has taken effect.","q":"Should the Court rule on the merits?","hint":"Who owns the prerogative being protected, and is there still something to stop?","alac":["No. The petition should be dismissed.","The Constitution limits judicial power to actual cases and controversies. Jurisprudence provides that individual senators have no standing to sue for a Senate prerogative unless the Senate as a body acts, and that a case becomes moot when the act sought to be stopped has already taken effect.","Here, the three senators sued on their own, with no Senate resolution asserting the prerogative. The withdrawal has also already taken effect after the notice period, so there is nothing left to stop.","Therefore, the petition should be dismissed for lack of standing and for being moot."],"key":"Before the merits, check standing (the Senate as a body) and mootness (has the withdrawal already taken effect?). Either can end the case."},{"c":"pangilinan","t":"twist","label":"Treaty backed by a later law","facts":"The Senate concurred in a climate change treaty in 2015 with no conditions. In 2018, Congress passed a law that adopts the treaty's targets, creates an agency to carry them out, and funds it. In 2026, the President withdraws from the treaty without consulting Congress.","q":"Is the withdrawal valid?","hint":"Look at what Congress did after the concurrence.","alac":["No. The President cannot withdraw alone.","The Constitution provides that no treaty is valid without the concurrence of at least two-thirds of all the Members of the Senate. Jurisprudence provides that the President generally may withdraw from a treaty, but not when Congress, after the concurrence, enacted a law that affirms or implements the treaty.","Here, Congress passed a law in 2018, after the Senate concurred in 2015, that adopts the treaty's targets and funds an agency to carry them out. Withdrawing alone would effectively set aside a law, which the President must faithfully execute.","Therefore, the President's withdrawal without Congress is invalid."],"key":"TWIST: a later implementing law locks the treaty in. Check the dates: the law must come AFTER the concurrence."},{"c":"colmenares","t":"same","label":"Suit to bare a loan deal","facts":"Journalists file a petition for mandamus against “the President and the Secretary of Finance” to make public a foreign loan agreement for a new railway. The Finance Secretary refuses, citing a confidentiality clause in the loan agreement.","q":"(a) Should the President be dropped as respondent? (b) Can the confidentiality clause prevent disclosure?","hint":"Two rules: immunity, and the duty to disclose information on foreign loans.","alac":["(a) Yes, the President should be dropped. (b) No, the confidentiality clause cannot prevent disclosure.","The Constitution provides that information on foreign loans obtained or guaranteed by the government shall be made available to the public. Jurisprudence provides that the sitting President is immune from suit during the term, but the case may continue against the other officials, and that a confidentiality clause cannot defeat the constitutional duty to disclose.","Here, the President is sitting, so the President must be dropped, but the Finance Secretary remains a proper respondent. The railway loan is a foreign loan, so information on it must be made public. A private clause in the contract cannot override the Constitution.","Therefore, the President is dropped, and the Finance Secretary must disclose the loan information."],"key":"Immunity protects only the President, not the Cabinet. And a contract clause never beats a constitutional duty to disclose."},{"c":"colmenares","t":"same","label":"Damages for acts before office","facts":"Two years before being elected, a candidate allegedly defamed a businessman at a rally. After the candidate becomes President, the businessman files a civil case for damages against the President in the RTC. He argues that immunity covers only official acts, and the defamation happened before the term.","q":"May the case proceed while the President is in office?","hint":"Does immunity depend on when or what the act was?","alac":["No. The case cannot proceed while the President is in office.","The Constitution vests the executive power in the President. Jurisprudence provides that the sitting President is immune from suit during the term, whether the case is civil or criminal and whether it concerns official or private acts, so that the President is not distracted from the duties of the office.","Here, the defendant is the sitting President. It does not matter that the remarks were made before the term, or that the case is civil. The immunity is tied to the office while the President holds it.","Therefore, the case cannot proceed now. The businessman may sue after the President's term ends."],"key":"Presidential immunity covers ANY case during the term. It is temporary, not a permanent shield."},{"c":"colmenares","t":"twist","label":"Suit after the term ends","facts":"A former President is charged with graft one year after her term ended. The charge concerns a kickback she allegedly received while in office from a contractor. She moves to dismiss, claiming presidential immunity because the acts happened during her term.","q":"Should the case be dismissed?","hint":"Is she still the sitting President?","alac":["No. The case should not be dismissed.","The Constitution vests the executive power in the President. Jurisprudence provides that presidential immunity lasts only during the term, and that a former President may be sued for unlawful acts committed while in office, since taking a bribe is not an official act.","Here, her term has already ended, so she is no longer a sitting President. Receiving a kickback is a crime, not part of her official duties. Immunity no longer protects her.","Therefore, the motion to dismiss should be denied."],"key":"TWIST: immunity ends with the term. A former President can be charged for crimes done in office."},{"c":"garafil","t":"same","label":"Oath after the ban starts","facts":"The election is on May 11. On February 20, the outgoing President signs Atty. L's appointment as Undersecretary, and it is released to her on March 2. She waits and takes her oath and assumes office on March 20. The new President later recalls her appointment as a midnight appointment.","q":"Is L's appointment valid?","hint":"List the four steps. Which step happened after the ban started?","alac":["No. L's appointment is void.","The Constitution provides that two months before the next presidential election and up to the end of the term, the President shall not make appointments, except temporary appointments to executive positions when continued vacancies would prejudice public service or endanger public safety. Jurisprudence provides that appointment is a process: signing, transmittal, a vacant position, and receipt and acceptance by the appointee. All steps must be completed before the ban starts.","Here, the ban began on March 11, two months before May 11. L's appointment was signed and released before the ban, but she accepted it by taking her oath and assuming office only on March 20, during the ban. The last step was incomplete when the ban started.","Therefore, L's appointment is void, and the recall is valid."],"key":"Count back two months from election day, then check every step. Acceptance (oath or assumption) counts too."},{"c":"garafil","t":"same","label":"Position not yet vacant","facts":"The election is on May 11. On March 5, the President signs and releases the appointment of Atty. D as Bureau Director, and D takes his oath on March 7. The current Director, however, retires only on March 15. D assumes office on March 16.","q":"Is D's appointment valid?","hint":"One of the four steps is about the position itself.","alac":["No. D's appointment is void.","The Constitution prohibits the President from making appointments two months before the next presidential election until the end of the term, except temporary appointments to executive positions when needed. Jurisprudence provides that a valid appointment requires the position to be vacant, along with signing, transmittal, and acceptance, and that all steps must be completed before the ban starts.","Here, the ban started on March 11. D's appointment was signed and accepted earlier, but the position was still occupied until March 15. An appointment to an office that is not yet vacant is not complete. By the time the position became vacant, the ban was already in effect.","Therefore, D's appointment is void."],"key":"Do not forget the vacancy step. Being signed and accepted early means nothing if someone still holds the office."},{"c":"garafil","t":"twist","label":"Temporary appointment in a crisis","facts":"The election is on May 11. On April 2, during an armed clash with rebels in Mindanao, the Secretary of National Defense dies. The outgoing President appoints General G as “Acting Secretary of National Defense” until the next President chooses a permanent Secretary. A taxpayer says this violates the appointments ban.","q":"Is G's appointment valid?","hint":"The ban has an exception. Read its words: temporary, executive, and what else?","alac":["Yes. G's appointment is valid.","The Constitution prohibits the President from making appointments two months before the next presidential election until the end of the term, except temporary appointments to executive positions when continued vacancies would prejudice public service or endanger public safety.","Here, the appointment was made during the ban, but it falls under the exception. The Defense Secretary is an executive position. The appointment is only temporary, until the next President chooses. Leaving the post empty during an armed clash would endanger public safety.","Therefore, G's temporary appointment is valid."],"key":"TWIST: the ban has a built-in exception. All three elements are needed: temporary + executive position + vacancy would prejudice service or endanger safety."},{"c":"decastro","t":"same","label":"RTC judge during the ban","facts":"The election is on May 11. An RTC judge in Cebu dies on March 20. The JBC submits a list of nominees on April 15, and the President appoints Judge J from the list on April 30. A losing nominee argues that the appointment violates the election ban.","q":"Is the appointment valid?","hint":"Which article is the ban in, and which branch does it cover?","alac":["Yes. The appointment is valid.","The Constitution prohibits the President from making appointments two months before the next presidential election, and separately requires that judges be appointed from at least three nominees prepared by the JBC. Jurisprudence provides that the appointments ban appears in the article on the Executive and covers only executive appointments, not appointments to the Judiciary.","Here, Judge J was appointed during the ban, but to the Judiciary, not the Executive. The JBC's list was followed. The JBC process already guards against partisan appointments, so the ban does not apply.","Therefore, the appointment of Judge J is valid."],"key":"Ban = Executive only. For the Judiciary, the check is the JBC list, not the election calendar."},{"c":"decastro","t":"same","label":"Supreme Court seat and the 90 days","facts":"An Associate Justice of the Supreme Court retires on April 1 of an election year, and the election is on May 11. The outgoing President's allies say she should leave the seat open for the next President because of the ban. Her critics agree. The JBC asks whether it should submit a list.","q":"Should the JBC submit a list, and may the outgoing President appoint?","hint":"What does the Constitution say about how fast Supreme Court vacancies must be filled?","alac":["Yes. The JBC should submit its list, and the outgoing President may appoint.","The Constitution provides that any vacancy in the Supreme Court shall be filled within 90 days from its occurrence, from a list of at least three nominees prepared by the JBC. Jurisprudence provides that the appointments ban covers only the Executive Department, so it does not stop the President from filling Supreme Court vacancies.","Here, the vacancy arose on April 1, during the ban. Since the ban does not cover the Judiciary, the 90-day command applies fully. Waiting for the next President could leave the seat empty beyond 90 days, against the Constitution's clear command.","Therefore, the JBC should submit its list, and the outgoing President may appoint."],"key":"Pair the two rules: the ban does not reach the Judiciary, and the 90-day period is mandatory (“shall”)."},{"c":"decastro","t":"twist","label":"Executive post during the ban","facts":"The election is on May 11. On April 10, the outgoing President gives a permanent appointment to Atty. V as Commissioner of the Bureau of Customs. There is no emergency, and an Officer-in-Charge is already running the bureau. Atty. V cites De Castro, arguing that the Court allowed appointments during the ban.","q":"Is V's appointment valid?","hint":"De Castro was about which branch?","alac":["No. V's appointment is void.","The Constitution prohibits the President from making appointments two months before the next presidential election until the end of the term, except temporary appointments to executive positions when continued vacancies would prejudice public service or endanger public safety. Jurisprudence provides that this ban covers appointments in the Executive Department.","Here, the Bureau of Customs is in the Executive. The appointment was made during the ban and is permanent, not temporary. There is no emergency, since an OIC already runs the bureau. The ruling that allows judicial appointments during the ban does not apply to executive posts.","Therefore, V's appointment is void."],"key":"TWIST: De Castro frees only the Judiciary. An executive appointment in the ban is void unless it fits the temporary-and-necessary exception."},{"c":"belgica","t":"same","label":"Oversight committee approves realignment","facts":"The GAA gives the DOH a lump sum for hospital upgrades. A special provision says the DOH may realign the fund between hospitals “only with the prior written approval of the Joint Congressional Health Oversight Committee.” The DOH wants to move funds to a typhoon-hit hospital, but the committee refuses.","q":"Is the special provision constitutional?","hint":"When does Congress's role in spending end?","alac":["No. The special provision is unconstitutional.","The Constitution separates the powers of Congress, which passes the budget, from the Executive, which spends it. Jurisprudence provides that once the budget law is passed, legislators may no longer take part in carrying it out, whether by identifying projects, realigning funds, or approving releases. Only oversight through hearings, inquiries, and the next budget is allowed.","Here, the provision requires a congressional committee to approve each realignment after the GAA has been passed. That gives legislators a say in carrying out the budget, which is the Executive's job. Refusing the DOH's request is exactly the kind of control after passage that is forbidden.","Therefore, the special provision is unconstitutional, and the DOH does not need the committee's approval."],"key":"Ask: is Congress acting BEFORE the budget passes (allowed) or AFTER (not allowed)? Post-enactment approval, realignment, or release by legislators is void."},{"c":"belgica","t":"same","label":"“Such other purposes as the President may determine”","facts":"A law creates a Mining Royalty Fund from royalties paid by mining companies. It states the fund “shall be used for rehabilitation of mined-out areas and for such other purposes as the President may determine.” The President uses part of it to build a convention center.","q":"Is the phrase “and for such other purposes as the President may determine” valid?","hint":"Does the phrase give the President any standard to follow?","alac":["No. The phrase is an undue delegation and is void. The first purpose remains valid.","The Constitution vests legislative power, including the power to decide how public funds are spent, in Congress. Jurisprudence provides that a law delegating power must be complete and must set a sufficient standard, and that a phrase allowing funds to be used for “such other purposes” as the President decides sets no standard at all.","Here, the use of the fund for rehabilitating mined-out areas is specific and valid. But the phrase “for such other purposes as the President may determine” lets the President decide any use, like a convention center. Congress did not fix the purpose, so it gave away its own power.","Therefore, the phrase is void, and the fund may be used only to rehabilitate mined-out areas."],"key":"Strike only the open-ended phrase. The specific purpose stays. Check the two tests: completeness and sufficient standard."},{"c":"belgica","t":"twist","label":"Senator inserts projects before passage","facts":"During deliberations on the budget bill, Senator S proposes specific line items: P300 million for a named bridge and P200 million for a named hospital in her province. Congress approves the GAA with these items, and the DPWH and DOH implement them. A taxpayer argues that this is “pork barrel,” citing Belgica.","q":"Are the line items constitutional?","hint":"Did S do anything after the GAA was passed?","alac":["Yes. The line items are constitutional.","The Constitution gives Congress the power to pass the budget, while the Executive carries it out. Jurisprudence provides that legislators may propose and amend items during budget deliberations, and what is prohibited is their participation after the budget has been passed, like identifying projects, realigning funds, or releasing money.","Here, Senator S proposed the bridge and hospital while the budget bill was still being deliberated. The items are specific and were approved by Congress as part of the law. After passage, the DPWH and DOH, not S, carried them out. She had no role after the budget was passed.","Therefore, the line items are valid, and the Belgica doctrine does not apply."],"key":"TWIST: specific items proposed BEFORE passage are legislation. The pork-barrel vice is control AFTER passage."},{"c":"sereno","t":"same","label":"Ombudsman short on practice years","facts":"The Ombudsman was appointed in 2022. In 2027, it is found that when appointed, she had only seven years of law practice, not the ten required by the Constitution. The Solicitor General files a quo warranto petition in the Supreme Court. She argues that, as an impeachable officer, she can be removed only by impeachment.","q":"Is quo warranto proper?","hint":"What does quo warranto question, and what does impeachment question?","alac":["Yes. Quo warranto is proper.","The Constitution provides that the Ombudsman must have been a judge or engaged in the practice of law for ten years or more, and lists the Ombudsman among impeachable officers who “may” be removed by impeachment. Jurisprudence provides that impeachment is not the only remedy, and that quo warranto may be filed to question whether an impeachable officer was eligible when appointed. Prescription does not run against the State.","Here, the question is whether she was qualified when appointed in 2022, not whether she misbehaved in office. Lacking the ten years of practice means she was not eligible. The Solicitor General filed in the Supreme Court on behalf of the State, so the passage of time does not bar the case.","Therefore, quo warranto is proper, and she may be ousted if found ineligible."],"key":"Quo warranto = ineligible at the START. Impeachment = misconduct DURING the term."},{"c":"sereno","t":"same","label":"COMELEC chair who just ran","facts":"The COMELEC Chairman was appointed in 2024. Four years later, it is found that he ran for and lost a congressional seat in the 2022 election, just before his appointment. The Solicitor General files quo warranto. He argues that (1) only impeachment can remove him and (2) the one-year period to file quo warranto lapsed long ago.","q":"Rule on both arguments.","hint":"Two issues: the proper remedy, and whether time bars the State.","alac":["Both arguments fail. Quo warranto is proper and is not barred by time.","The Constitution provides that members of the COMELEC must not have been candidates for any elective position in the immediately preceding election, and that they may be removed by impeachment. Jurisprudence provides that quo warranto may be used against impeachable officers to question their eligibility at appointment, and that the one-year period does not apply when the State, through the Solicitor General, files the case.","Here, he ran in 2022, the election just before his 2024 appointment, so he was disqualified when appointed. That is a question of eligibility, which quo warranto covers. The Solicitor General filed for the State, so the four-year delay does not matter.","Therefore, quo warranto is proper, and he may be ousted."],"key":"Answer both prongs: (1) impeachment is not exclusive; (2) prescription does not run against the State."},{"c":"sereno","t":"twist","label":"Justice takes bribes in office","facts":"Justice J was fully qualified when appointed in 2018. In 2026, evidence shows she accepted bribes to decide cases. Instead of starting impeachment, the Solicitor General files a quo warranto petition to oust her, citing Republic v. Sereno.","q":"Is quo warranto the proper remedy?","hint":"Was she eligible when appointed?","alac":["No. Quo warranto is not proper. The remedy is impeachment.","The Constitution provides that Members of the Supreme Court may be removed from office on impeachment for, among others, bribery, graft and corruption, and betrayal of public trust. Jurisprudence provides that quo warranto covers only eligibility at the time of appointment, while acts committed during the term are grounds for impeachment.","Here, J was fully qualified when appointed in 2018. The charge is bribery committed while in office, which is misconduct during the term, not a problem with her eligibility. Sereno does not apply because there is no question about whether she was eligible to be appointed.","Therefore, quo warranto is not proper, and she must be removed, if at all, by impeachment."],"key":"TWIST: Sereno does not swallow impeachment. Misconduct in office → impeachment only."},{"c":"jardeleza","t":"same","label":"Anonymous bribery letter","facts":"Judge A applies for a CA seat. The day before the vote, a JBC member reads an anonymous letter accusing A of taking bribes and invokes the unanimity rule. A is never told about the letter or asked to answer it, and she is left off the shortlist. She learns of it from news reports.","q":"Did the JBC act properly?","hint":"What must the JBC give an applicant whose integrity is questioned?","alac":["No. The JBC violated A's right to due process.","The Constitution provides that a Member of the Judiciary must be a person of proven competence, integrity, probity, and independence, and that the JBC recommends appointees. Jurisprudence provides that although JBC proceedings are sui generis, an applicant whose integrity is challenged must be told of the specific charges and given a real chance to answer them before being excluded.","Here, the JBC relied on an anonymous letter to question A's integrity. A was never informed of it or allowed to respond, and she found out only from the news. She was excluded without any chance to defend herself.","Therefore, the JBC acted improperly, and A's exclusion violated due process."],"key":"Integrity challenge → notice of the specific charge + a real chance to answer. Learning about it after the list is out is not due process."},{"c":"jardeleza","t":"same","label":"Disagreement over a dissent","facts":"Justice B of the Court of Appeals applies to the Supreme Court. A JBC member objects because B wrote a dissent favoring a narrow reading of a free speech law, calling it “an integrity problem.” The JBC applies the unanimity rule, and B falls one vote short.","q":"Was the unanimity rule properly applied?","hint":"Is a legal view the same as a question of integrity?","alac":["No. The unanimity rule did not apply.","The Constitution requires the JBC to recommend persons of proven competence, integrity, probity, and independence. Jurisprudence provides that the JBC's unanimity rule applies only to real questions of integrity, and that a disagreement with an applicant's legal position or strategy is not an integrity issue.","Here, the objection is about B's legal reasoning in a dissent. That shows a different view of the law, not dishonesty or lack of moral character. Calling it an integrity problem does not make it one.","Therefore, the unanimity rule was wrongly applied, and B's votes should be counted under the regular majority rule."],"key":"Legal views ≠ integrity. If the objection is about how someone reads the law, the unanimity rule cannot be used."},{"c":"jardeleza","t":"twist","label":"Charges given, hearing held","facts":"The JBC tells applicant C in writing that she is accused of falsifying her service records. It gives her the documents and a hearing date. C appears with counsel and answers. After the hearing, the JBC, applying the unanimity rule, excludes her from the list. C argues that she was denied due process.","q":"Was C denied due process?","hint":"Due process guarantees a chance to be heard, not a particular result.","alac":["No. C was not denied due process.","The Constitution requires the JBC to recommend persons of proven integrity. Jurisprudence provides that when the JBC questions an applicant's integrity, it must give notice of the specific charges and a real chance to answer, but due process does not guarantee a favorable result.","Here, C was told in writing of the specific charge, given the documents, and heard with counsel. Falsifying service records is a true integrity issue, so the unanimity rule applies. The JBC then decided after hearing her side.","Therefore, C was given due process, and her exclusion stands."],"key":"TWIST: if notice + hearing were given and the issue is real integrity, the exclusion stands. Due process is about the chance to be heard, not winning."},{"c":"villanueva","t":"same","label":"Exam and training scores","facts":"The JBC publishes a policy requiring applicants for RTC judge to pass a psychological exam and to score at least 80% in its pre-judicature training program. Lawyer L, who meets all the minimum qualifications set by the Constitution and the law, scores 72% and is not considered. He argues the JBC cannot add requirements.","q":"Is the policy valid?","hint":"Is the JBC allowed to measure competence beyond the minimum?","alac":["Yes. The policy is valid.","The Constitution provides that Members of the Judiciary must be of proven competence, integrity, probity, and independence, and that the JBC's main function is to recommend appointees. Jurisprudence provides that the JBC may set reasonable standards beyond the minimum qualifications to measure these traits, provided its policies are published, and that no applicant has a vested right to be nominated.","Here, the exam and training score help measure competence and fitness. The policy was published, so applicants knew of it. L meeting the minimum qualifications does not give him a right to be nominated.","Therefore, the policy is valid, and L's exclusion stands."],"key":"Minimum qualifications let you apply. The JBC can still set reasonable, published standards to rank and screen."},{"c":"villanueva","t":"same","label":"Pending administrative cases","facts":"The JBC publishes a rule that applicants with pending administrative cases will not be considered until the cases are resolved. Judge K, who has a pending administrative complaint for delay, is not included in the list for a CA seat. He claims the rule violates equal protection because he is presumed innocent.","q":"Is the rule valid?","hint":"Is the classification reasonable and related to fitness for office?","alac":["Yes. The rule is valid.","The Constitution requires that judges be of proven competence, integrity, probity, and independence, and guarantees equal protection of the laws. Jurisprudence provides that the JBC may adopt reasonable, published standards for evaluating applicants, and that a classification is valid if it rests on real differences related to the purpose.","Here, applicants with pending administrative cases differ from those with clean records, because their fitness is still being questioned. The rule is related to the goal of choosing judges of proven integrity. It was published and applies to everyone in the same situation.","Therefore, the rule is valid, and K's exclusion does not violate equal protection."],"key":"Presumption of innocence is for criminal cases. For the JBC, the question is whether the standard is reasonable and related to fitness."},{"c":"villanueva","t":"twist","label":"Only three law schools","facts":"The JBC publishes a new policy: only graduates of three named law schools may be considered for the Court of Appeals, “to ensure quality.” Justice M, a top-performing RTC judge who graduated from a provincial law school, is automatically excluded.","q":"Is the policy valid?","hint":"The JBC may add standards, but must they be reasonable?","alac":["No. The policy is invalid.","The Constitution guarantees equal protection of the laws and requires judges to be of proven competence, integrity, probity, and independence. Jurisprudence provides that the JBC may adopt standards beyond the minimum qualifications, but they must be reasonable, meaning based on real differences that are related to fitness for judicial office.","Here, the policy excludes applicants based only on the school they graduated from, not on their actual competence or record. M is a top-performing judge, yet she is shut out automatically. The school one attended does not by itself show competence or integrity.","Therefore, the policy is an arbitrary classification and is invalid."],"key":"TWIST: the JBC's power to add standards is not unlimited. Each standard must be reasonable and tied to competence, integrity, probity, or independence."},{"c":"chavez","t":"same","label":"Law expands the JBC to nine","facts":"Congress passes a law adding two more seats to the JBC, one for the Senate Majority Leader and one for the House Majority Leader, in addition to the one Congress representative already sitting. The JBC would have nine members.","q":"Is the law valid?","hint":"Who fixed the JBC's membership?","alac":["No. The law is unconstitutional.","The Constitution creates the JBC with the Chief Justice as chair, the Secretary of Justice, and a representative of Congress as ex officio members, and four regular members. Jurisprudence provides that “a representative of Congress” means only one, and that the seven-member composition is fixed by the Constitution and cannot be changed by law.","Here, the law adds two more representatives of Congress, giving it three seats and the JBC nine members. That goes beyond the single representative the Constitution allows and changes the body's composition.","Therefore, the law is unconstitutional."],"key":"JBC = 7 members, 1 from Congress. Any law adding seats is void."},{"c":"chavez","t":"same","label":"Attack on old nominations","facts":"After the Court rules that Congress may have only one JBC representative, a lawyer files a petition to void all appointments to the Judiciary made from JBC lists prepared while two Congress representatives sat in the JBC.","q":"Should the past appointments be voided?","hint":"What doctrine protects acts done under a rule later struck down?","alac":["No. The past appointments remain valid.","The Constitution allows only one representative of Congress in the JBC. Jurisprudence provides that, under the operative fact doctrine, acts done in good faith under a rule later declared unconstitutional remain valid, to avoid unfair and disruptive results.","Here, the JBC prepared its lists while two Congress representatives sat in good faith. Many judges were appointed and have decided cases since. Voiding all of them would cause chaos and harm people who relied on those acts.","Therefore, the past appointments remain valid under the operative fact doctrine."],"key":"Unconstitutional going forward; past acts are protected by the operative fact doctrine."},{"c":"chavez","t":"twist","label":"Senate and House take turns","facts":"After the Chavez ruling, the Senate and the House agree that one representative of Congress will sit in the JBC with one full vote, and the seat will rotate between a Senator and a House Member every six months. A lawyer argues that this is a way around Chavez.","q":"Is the rotation valid?","hint":"How many representatives sit, and how many votes do they cast at any time?","alac":["Yes. The rotation is valid.","The Constitution provides that the JBC shall include a representative of Congress. Jurisprudence provides that this means only one representative with one vote, and that Congress may decide who its representative will be.","Here, at any given time, there is only one representative of Congress casting one full vote. The JBC keeps its seven members. Taking turns is simply Congress's way of choosing its representative, which is its own decision to make.","Therefore, the rotation is valid and does not violate Chavez."],"key":"TWIST: Chavez forbids two seats, not taking turns. One person, one vote at a time is fine."},{"c":"aguinaldo","t":"same","label":"Two Supreme Court seats at once","facts":"Two Supreme Court Justices retire on the same day. The JBC sends two separate lists: List 1 for “Seat A” and List 2 for “Seat B.” The President appoints both new Justices from List 1. A nominee on List 2 who was not appointed sues.","q":"Are the appointments valid?","hint":"May the JBC split nominees into separate lists for vacancies that open at the same time?","alac":["Yes. Both appointments are valid.","The Constitution provides that the President shall appoint Members of the Judiciary from a list of at least three nominees prepared by the JBC for every vacancy. Jurisprudence provides that the JBC cannot cluster nominees into separate lists for vacancies that exist at the same time, and that the President may choose from all the nominees.","Here, the two vacancies arose on the same day, so they existed at the same time. Splitting the nominees into two lists had no basis and limited the President's choice. The President could choose from all the nominees, including picking both from List 1.","Therefore, the appointments are valid, and the List 2 nominee's suit fails."],"key":"Simultaneous vacancies = one combined pool. Clustering limits the President's power to appoint."},{"c":"aguinaldo","t":"same","label":"Fight over seniority","facts":"Three Sandiganbayan seats open at the same time. The JBC labels its lists as Seat 1, 2, and 3. The President signs Justice Z's appointment first, then X, then Y. X was on the “Seat 1” list and claims to be the most senior.","q":"Who is the most senior among the three?","hint":"Does the cluster label decide anything?","alac":["Justice Z is the most senior.","The Constitution gives the President the power to appoint Members of the Judiciary from JBC nominees. Jurisprudence provides that clustering nominees for simultaneous vacancies has no legal effect, and that seniority among Justices appointed for these vacancies follows the order in which they were appointed.","Here, the seats opened at the same time, so the labels on the JBC lists mean nothing. The President signed Z's appointment first, then X, then Y. X's place on the “Seat 1” list does not make her senior.","Therefore, Z is the most senior, followed by X, then Y."],"key":"Cluster labels are void, so they cannot decide seniority. Order of appointment does."},{"c":"aguinaldo","t":"twist","label":"Vacancies at different times","facts":"A CA seat opens in January. The JBC sends a list in February, and in March the President appoints Justice H from it. In June, another CA seat opens, and the JBC opens new applications and sends a new list in July. Atty. N, who was on the February list but not chosen, did not reapply. He asks to be appointed to the June seat, citing Aguinaldo: “the President may choose from all nominees.”","q":"May the President appoint N to the June seat from the February list?","hint":"Did the two vacancies ever exist at the same time?","alac":["No. N cannot be appointed to the June seat from the February list.","The Constitution provides that the President shall appoint Members of the Judiciary from a list of at least three nominees prepared by the JBC for every vacancy. Jurisprudence prohibits clustering and allows the President to choose from all nominees only when the vacancies exist at the same time; when vacancies arise at different times, each vacancy has its own list.","Here, the January seat was filled in March, before the June seat opened. The two vacancies never existed at the same time. The February list was for the January vacancy only, and it was used up when H was appointed. N did not reapply, so he is not on the July list for the June seat.","Therefore, the President must choose from the July list, and N cannot be appointed."],"key":"TWIST: “choose from all nominees” applies only to vacancies that exist at the same time. A separate, later vacancy has its own list."},{"c":"recoa","t":"same","label":"Retiring judges buy service cars","facts":"Under guidelines approved by the Supreme Court, retiring RTC judges may buy the service vehicles assigned to them at a value computed by the Court's Property Division. COA disallows the sales, saying the price should have been computed using COA's appraisal formula, which yields a higher amount.","q":"Must the Court follow COA's formula?","hint":"What does fiscal autonomy allow the Judiciary to do with its own property?","alac":["No. The Court's guidelines prevail.","The Constitution grants the Judiciary fiscal autonomy and gives COA the power to audit government accounts. Jurisprudence provides that fiscal autonomy allows the Judiciary to use and dispose of its own resources under its own guidelines, and that COA, while it may audit, cannot impose its own valuation formula on how the Court disposes of its own property.","Here, the vehicles belong to the Judiciary and were sold under guidelines the Supreme Court itself approved. Forcing the Court to use COA's formula would let COA control the Court's decisions on its own property, which undermines fiscal autonomy and judicial independence.","Therefore, the Court need not follow COA's formula, and the sales are valid."],"key":"COA may audit, but cannot dictate. Fiscal autonomy means the Judiciary decides how to use and dispose of its own resources."},{"c":"recoa","t":"same","label":"Disposal of old court equipment","facts":"The Supreme Court issues guidelines for selling unserviceable office equipment from its trial courts to their employees, at prices set by the Court's own committee. COA issues a memo saying all such sales must use its formula and that its regional auditors must approve each sale in advance.","q":"Is the COA memo binding on the Judiciary?","hint":"There is a difference between auditing and controlling.","alac":["No. The COA memo is not binding on how the Judiciary disposes of its property.","The Constitution grants the Judiciary fiscal autonomy and independence, and gives COA the power to audit. Jurisprudence provides that the Judiciary may manage and dispose of its own resources under its own guidelines, and that COA cannot force its own formula or approval process on the Court.","Here, COA wants its formula used and its auditors to approve each sale in advance. That gives COA control over decisions that belong to the Judiciary. The Court already adopted its own guidelines for these sales.","Therefore, the COA memo cannot bind the Judiciary, though COA may still audit the transactions afterward."],"key":"Prior approval and forced formulas = control (not allowed). Post-audit = allowed."},{"c":"recoa","t":"twist","label":"Court refuses a regular audit","facts":"COA schedules a regular post-audit of the Judiciary Development Fund's expenses for the year. A court administrator refuses to release the records, saying that the Judiciary's fiscal autonomy and the Re: COA Opinion ruling exempt it from COA audit.","q":"Is the refusal proper?","hint":"Did the case say that COA cannot audit at all?","alac":["No. The refusal is improper.","The Constitution gives COA the power to examine and audit all accounts of the government, including constitutional bodies with fiscal autonomy, and grants the Judiciary fiscal autonomy. Jurisprudence provides that fiscal autonomy allows the Judiciary to manage its own resources, but it does not exempt the Judiciary from COA's audit.","Here, COA is conducting a regular post-audit of fund expenses. It is not imposing a formula or controlling decisions in advance. This is the very audit power the Constitution gives COA.","Therefore, the court administrator must release the records."],"key":"TWIST: Re: COA Opinion stops COA from DICTATING, not from AUDITING. Fiscal autonomy is not freedom from audit."},{"c":"jdf","t":"same","label":"Bill to abolish a court","facts":"A House Member files a bill to abolish the Sandiganbayan and transfer its cases to the RTCs. The bill is still with the committee and has not been heard. Lawyers file a petition with the Supreme Court to declare the bill unconstitutional and stop Congress from acting on it.","q":"Should the Court rule on the petition?","hint":"Is there an actual case yet?","alac":["No. The petition should be dismissed.","The Constitution limits judicial power to settling actual controversies involving rights that are legally demandable and enforceable. Jurisprudence provides that a bill pending in Congress is not law and creates no actual case, so the Court cannot review it.","Here, the bill is still in committee and has not even been heard. No one's rights have been affected, and it may never become law. Any ruling would be an advisory opinion.","Therefore, the Court should dismiss the petition for lack of an actual case."],"key":"No law yet = no actual case. Wait until it is enacted and applied."},{"c":"jdf","t":"same","label":"Pending charter-change resolution","facts":"The House files a resolution calling Congress to convene as a constituent assembly to amend the Constitution, with both houses voting jointly. The resolution has been referred to committee but not adopted. Citizens ask the Supreme Court to declare joint voting unconstitutional.","q":"Is the petition ripe for decision?","hint":"Has anything actually been done that affects anyone?","alac":["No. The petition is premature.","The Constitution limits judicial power to actual cases and controversies. Jurisprudence provides that a question is ripe only when the act being challenged has been done and has directly affected someone, so a mere proposal in Congress cannot be reviewed.","Here, the resolution is still in committee and has not been adopted. Congress has not convened, and no vote has taken place. Whether joint voting will happen is still uncertain.","Therefore, the petition should be dismissed as premature."],"key":"Proposals, bills, and resolutions not yet acted upon are not ripe. Courts wait for an actual act."},{"c":"jdf","t":"twist","label":"The bill becomes law","facts":"A bill becomes law, removing the Judiciary's power to collect and use certain court fees and requiring the fees to be remitted to the National Treasury. As a result, court employees' allowances funded by the fees are stopped. The employees' association sues.","q":"May the Court now rule on the law?","hint":"What changed from when it was just a bill?","alac":["Yes. The Court may now rule.","The Constitution limits judicial power to actual cases and grants the Judiciary fiscal autonomy. Jurisprudence provides that there is an actual, ripe case once a law has been enacted and has directly injured the party challenging it.","Here, the bill is now law, and it has been applied. Court employees' allowances have actually stopped, so the employees suffered direct injury and have standing. The issue is no longer a proposal.","Therefore, the Court may rule on whether the law is constitutional."],"key":"TWIST: once enacted and applied, with someone actually hurt, the case is ripe. Then the merits (fiscal autonomy) can be argued."},{"c":"castaneda","t":"same","label":"Clerk resigns mid-case","facts":"A complaint for dishonesty is filed against Clerk of Court C in January. In March, while the case is pending, C resigns. She argues that the Court has lost jurisdiction since she is no longer a court employee.","q":"Did the Court lose jurisdiction?","hint":"When was the complaint filed?","alac":["No. The Court did not lose jurisdiction.","The Constitution gives the Supreme Court administrative supervision over all courts and their personnel. Jurisprudence provides that once an administrative complaint is filed while the respondent is still in service, the Court keeps jurisdiction even if the respondent later resigns or retires.","Here, the complaint was filed in January, while C was still Clerk of Court. Her resignation in March came after jurisdiction attached. Resigning cannot be used to escape liability.","Therefore, the Court keeps jurisdiction and may impose penalties such as a fine or forfeiture of benefits."],"key":"Filed while in service → jurisdiction stays, even after resignation or retirement."},{"c":"castaneda","t":"same","label":"Judge dies while case pending","facts":"An administrative complaint against Judge D for gross ignorance of the law is pending. The OCA recommends dismissal from service with forfeiture of benefits. Before the Court decides, Judge D dies. The complainant asks the Court to still impose the penalty.","q":"Should the Court impose the penalty?","hint":"What happens to a pending case when the respondent dies?","alac":["No. The complaint should be dismissed.","The Constitution gives the Supreme Court administrative supervision over all courts and their personnel. Jurisprudence provides that the death of the respondent while an administrative case is pending generally calls for its dismissal, since the respondent can no longer defend himself and any penalty would burden his heirs, unless an exception applies.","Here, Judge D died before the Court decided. There is no showing of an exception, such as a denial of due process, equitable or humanitarian reasons, or a penalty that would still make sense. Forfeiting his benefits would fall on his heirs.","Therefore, the complaint should be dismissed."],"key":"Death ≠ resignation. Death generally ends the case, unless an exception applies."},{"c":"castaneda","t":"twist","label":"Complaint filed after retirement","facts":"Judge R retires in January 2026. In March 2026, a litigant files an administrative complaint against him for a ruling he made in 2025. The OCA recommends a fine.","q":"May the Court still take the case?","hint":"Compare with a case filed BEFORE retirement.","alac":["No. The Court has no administrative jurisdiction over the complaint.","The Constitution gives the Supreme Court administrative supervision over courts and their personnel. Jurisprudence provides that administrative jurisdiction attaches only if the complaint is filed while the respondent is still in service.","Here, Judge R had already retired in January when the complaint was filed in March. Since he was no longer in service, jurisdiction never attached, even though the act complained of happened while he was a judge. Other remedies, such as civil or criminal action, may still be available.","Therefore, the Court cannot take the administrative case."],"key":"TWIST: the timing of FILING matters, not the timing of the act. Filed after separation = no administrative jurisdiction."},{"c":"caronan","t":"same","label":"Someone else took the bar exam","facts":"Atty. E was admitted to the bar in 2018. In 2026, evidence shows that E's twin brother took the bar exam in E's name. E says he has practiced honestly for eight years and that his admission is final.","q":"Should E remain a lawyer?","hint":"Can admission based on fraud ever become valid?","alac":["No. E's name should be stricken from the Roll of Attorneys.","The Constitution gives the Supreme Court the power to promulgate rules on admission to the practice of law. Jurisprudence provides that the practice of law is a privilege granted only to those who meet the Court's requirements, and that admission obtained by fraud is void from the start.","Here, E did not take the bar exam himself; his twin did. E's admission rests on fraud, so it was void from the beginning. Eight years of honest practice cannot cure a void admission.","Therefore, E's name should be stricken from the Roll of Attorneys."],"key":"Fraud makes admission void. Time and good performance do not cure it."},{"c":"caronan","t":"same","label":"Hidden criminal conviction","facts":"In her petition to take the bar exam, Atty. F answered “No” to the question asking whether she had ever been convicted of a crime. In fact, she had been convicted of estafa three years earlier. She passed the bar and has practiced for two years.","q":"May her admission be revoked?","hint":"What does the privilege of practicing law require besides passing the exam?","alac":["Yes. Her admission may be revoked.","The Constitution gives the Supreme Court the power to promulgate rules on admission to the practice of law. Jurisprudence provides that the practice of law is a privilege granted only to those who meet the requirements of learning and good moral character, and that admission obtained by fraud or concealment is void.","Here, F concealed a conviction for estafa, a crime involving dishonesty, when asked directly. That concealment kept the Court from assessing her moral fitness. Her admission rests on a false statement.","Therefore, her admission may be revoked, and her name stricken from the Roll."],"key":"Moral fitness is part of the requirements. Concealing facts that bear on it is fraud on the Court."},{"c":"caronan","t":"twist","label":"Honest clerical error","facts":"Atty. G discovers that his law school transcript lists his middle name as “Santos” instead of “Santo.” Everything else is accurate, and he personally finished his law degree and took the bar exam. A rival lawyer files a complaint, citing Caronan, saying G's admission is void.","q":"Is G's admission void?","hint":"Was there any fraud?","alac":["No. G's admission is valid.","The Constitution gives the Supreme Court the power over admission to the practice of law. Jurisprudence provides that admission obtained by fraud is void, but an honest clerical error, without any deceit, does not make admission void.","Here, G himself completed law school and took the bar exam. The only issue is a one-letter mistake in his middle name, with no intent to deceive and no benefit gained. Unlike in Caronan, there is no use of another person's identity or records.","Therefore, G's admission is valid, and he only needs to have the record corrected."],"key":"TWIST: Caronan requires FRAUD. A clerical mistake without deceit does not void admission."},{"c":"brillantes","t":"same","label":"Acting COA Chair","facts":"The COA Chairperson resigns. While choosing a permanent replacement, the President designates COA Commissioner T as “Acting Chairperson,” revocable at the President's pleasure. A taxpayer challenges the designation.","q":"Is the designation valid?","hint":"Whose pleasure would T serve?","alac":["No. The designation is unconstitutional.","The Constitution establishes COA as an independent Constitutional Commission. Jurisprudence provides that the President cannot designate an acting chairman of a Constitutional Commission, even temporarily, because a designation the President can revoke at will makes the designee serve at the President's pleasure.","Here, the President designated T as Acting Chairperson, revocable at the President's pleasure. This makes the head of COA, which audits the Executive, dependent on the President. The choice of a temporary head belongs to the Commission itself.","Therefore, the designation is invalid."],"key":"The President may APPOINT a Chair (with CA confirmation), but may not DESIGNATE an acting one."},{"c":"brillantes","t":"same","label":"Undersecretary as COMELEC OIC","facts":"The COMELEC Chairman goes on a three-month medical leave. The President designates an Undersecretary of the DILG as “Officer-in-Charge of the COMELEC” until the Chairman returns.","q":"Is the designation valid?","hint":"Two problems: who designated, and who was designated.","alac":["No. The designation is unconstitutional.","The Constitution establishes the COMELEC as an independent Constitutional Commission. Jurisprudence provides that the President cannot designate anyone as acting head of a Constitutional Commission, because doing so places the Commission under the President's control.","Here, the President designated an executive official, a DILG Undersecretary, to run the COMELEC. This is worse than designating a Commissioner, since an outsider from the Executive would control an independent body. The COMELEC itself should decide who acts as head during the Chairman's leave.","Therefore, the designation is invalid."],"key":"Independence means the Executive cannot put its own person in charge, even for a short time."},{"c":"brillantes","t":"twist","label":"Commission picks its own acting head","facts":"The CSC Chairperson goes on a two-month leave. The CSC, meeting en banc, passes a resolution designating its most senior Commissioner as Acting Chairperson during the leave. An employee argues that this violates Brillantes v. Yorac.","q":"Is the resolution valid?","hint":"Who made the designation in Brillantes, and who made it here?","alac":["Yes. The resolution is valid.","The Constitution establishes the CSC as an independent Constitutional Commission. Jurisprudence provides that the President cannot designate an acting chairman, because the choice of a temporary head belongs to the Commission itself.","Here, it was the CSC en banc, not the President, that designated the acting head. It chose its own senior Commissioner for a short leave. This keeps the Commission independent from the Executive.","Therefore, the resolution is valid and does not violate Brillantes."],"key":"TWIST: Brillantes is about WHO designates. The Commission choosing its own acting head is fine."},{"c":"funa","t":"same","label":"Commissioner made Chair after term expires","facts":"The CSC Chair's seven-year term expires normally on February 2, 2026. The President appoints Commissioner U, who has already served four years, as the new Chair “for a full term of seven years.”","q":"Is U's appointment valid?","hint":"What kind of vacancy is this, and what term does it call for?","alac":["No. U's appointment is void.","The Constitution provides that Members of the Constitutional Commissions serve a term of seven years without reappointment, and that a person appointed to a vacancy serves only the unexpired term. Jurisprudence provides that when a term expires, the successor must be appointed to a full seven-year term, and that total service in a Commission can never exceed seven years.","Here, the vacancy was caused by the expiration of the previous Chair's term, so the new Chair must get a full seven years. U has already served four years, so seven more years would give U eleven years in total. That is a prohibited reappointment.","Therefore, U's appointment is void."],"key":"Expired term → full 7 years → a sitting Commissioner can never fill it, since total service would exceed 7."},{"c":"funa","t":"same","label":"Successor given too long a term","facts":"A COMELEC Commissioner dies with three years left in her term. The President appoints Atty. W, who has never served in the COMELEC, to the vacant seat “for a term of seven years.”","q":"How long is W's term?","hint":"Why is the seat vacant?","alac":["W serves only the remaining three years.","The Constitution provides that a person appointed to a vacancy in a Constitutional Commission serves only for the unexpired term of the predecessor. Jurisprudence provides that when the vacancy is caused by death, resignation, disability, or impeachment, the appointee serves only the remainder of the term.","Here, the seat is vacant because the Commissioner died, with three years left. W is appointed to fill that vacancy, so W gets only the unexpired portion. The words “for a term of seven years” cannot extend it.","Therefore, W serves only three years. The excess in the appointment is void."],"key":"Vacancy from death, resignation, disability, or impeachment → only the leftover term, no matter what the appointment paper says."},{"c":"funa","t":"twist","label":"Commissioner fills an unexpired term","facts":"COA Commissioner Y has served two years. The COA Chair dies with four years left in his term. The President appoints Y as Chair for the remaining four years. A taxpayer, citing Funa, argues that a Commissioner can never become Chair.","q":"Is Y's appointment valid?","hint":"Add up Y's total years of service.","alac":["Yes. Y's appointment is valid.","The Constitution provides that Members of the Constitutional Commissions serve seven years without reappointment, and that an appointee to a vacancy serves only the unexpired term. Jurisprudence provides that a Commissioner may be appointed Chair to fill a vacancy caused by death, resignation, disability, or impeachment, but only for the unexpired term, and only if total service does not exceed seven years.","Here, the vacancy was caused by the Chair's death. Y is appointed only for the remaining four years. Y's two years as Commissioner plus four years as Chair add up to six years, which is within seven.","Therefore, Y's appointment is valid."],"key":"TWIST: Funa does not ban promotion completely. It is allowed if (1) the vacancy is not from term expiry, (2) only for the unexpired term, and (3) total ≤ 7 years."},{"c":"pililla","t":"same","label":"Governor's private secretary","facts":"Governor H appoints S as his private secretary. S handles the governor's confidential mail and sits in closed-door meetings. After a falling out, H tells S he has lost confidence in her and appoints someone else. S argues that she can be removed only for cause after a hearing.","q":"Is S's separation valid?","hint":"What happens to the term of a primarily confidential employee when trust is lost?","alac":["Yes. S's separation is valid.","The Constitution provides that no officer or employee of the civil service shall be removed except for cause provided by law, but it treats primarily confidential positions separately. Jurisprudence provides that whether a position is primarily confidential is decided by its actual duties, and that the term of a primarily confidential employee ends upon loss of confidence, which is not removal.","Here, S handles confidential mail and attends closed-door meetings, so her work is close and personal to the governor. Her position is primarily confidential. When H lost confidence in her, her term simply ended. There was no removal, so no cause or hearing was needed.","Therefore, S's separation is valid."],"key":"Loss of confidence = end of term, not removal. So the “for cause” rule does not apply."},{"c":"pililla","t":"same","label":"Water district GM after a CSC ruling","facts":"A water district Board, which appoints and works closely with the General Manager, reappoints its retired GM under a coterminous status. The CSC disapproves the appointment, citing its own circular that classifies the GM post as career service.","q":"Is the CSC correct?","hint":"Who has the final say on whether a position is primarily confidential?","alac":["No. The coterminous appointment is valid.","The Constitution classifies civil service positions into career and non-career, with primarily confidential positions treated separately. Jurisprudence provides that the GM of a water district holds a primarily confidential position because the GM serves at the pleasure of the Board and works closely with it, and that the courts, not the CSC, have the final say based on the nature of the duties.","Here, the GM is appointed by the Board and works closely with it. The nature of the job makes it primarily confidential, whatever the CSC circular says. A coterminous appointment fits a position that lasts only as long as the Board's trust.","Therefore, the CSC is incorrect, and the appointment is valid."],"key":"Labels do not decide. The courts look at actual duties (closeness and trust)."},{"c":"pililla","t":"twist","label":"Driver labeled “confidential”","facts":"A city's plantilla lists the position of motor pool driver as “primarily confidential.” The driver drives any official who needs a vehicle and does no other work. A new mayor declares loss of confidence and replaces him.","q":"Is the driver's separation valid?","hint":"Look at what the driver actually does.","alac":["No. The separation is invalid.","The Constitution provides that no officer or employee of the civil service shall be removed except for cause provided by law. Jurisprudence provides that whether a position is primarily confidential depends on its actual duties, especially a close and trusted relationship with the appointing authority, and not on how it is labeled.","Here, the driver serves any official who needs a car from the motor pool. He has no close, personal relationship with the mayor and handles no confidential matters. Calling the position “confidential” on paper does not make it so.","Therefore, the driver is not a confidential employee, his “loss of confidence” separation is really removal without cause, and it is invalid."],"key":"TWIST: same test, opposite result. If the duties are routine and impersonal, the label does not help. Removal needs cause."},{"c":"ppi","t":"same","label":"Free space in magazines","facts":"COMELEC orders all weekly magazines to give one free page in each issue during the campaign for its voter education ads. Magazine publishers object.","q":"Is the order valid?","hint":"What is COMELEC taking from the publishers?","alac":["No. The order is invalid.","The Constitution provides that private property shall not be taken for public use without just compensation, and gives COMELEC the power to supervise or regulate media during elections. Jurisprudence provides that forcing print media to donate space is a taking of private property without just compensation, and that the power to supervise or regulate does not include a power to take.","Here, the magazines' pages are their private property and their source of income. COMELEC is requiring them to give a full page for free in every issue. No necessity was shown, and no payment is offered.","Therefore, the order is invalid."],"key":"Print space = private property. Free space by order = taking without just compensation."},{"c":"ppi","t":"same","label":"Free banners on news websites","facts":"COMELEC requires all private online news websites to display a free “COMELEC Space” banner on their homepages for 90 days, showing candidates' platforms. Website owners say this costs them advertising revenue.","q":"Is the requirement valid?","hint":"Is a website more like a newspaper or a broadcast station that uses public airwaves?","alac":["No. The requirement is invalid.","The Constitution provides that private property shall not be taken for public use without just compensation. Jurisprudence provides that COMELEC cannot compel privately owned media to give free space, because this is a taking without just compensation, unlike broadcasters who use public airwaves under a government franchise.","Here, the websites are privately owned and do not use franchised airwaves. Their homepage space produces advertising income, like newspaper space. COMELEC is taking it for 90 days without paying.","Therefore, the requirement is invalid."],"key":"The key difference is franchise and public airwaves. Without them, it is like a newspaper: no free space by force."},{"c":"ppi","t":"twist","label":"Free airtime on radio","facts":"Under the election law, COMELEC orders all radio stations to give 30 minutes of free airtime daily during the campaign for candidates to present their platforms. Radio stations, citing PPI v. COMELEC, say this is also a taking without just compensation.","q":"Is the order valid?","hint":"Who owns the airwaves the stations use?","alac":["Yes. The order is valid.","The Constitution allows the State to regulate franchises, which are always subject to amendment for the common good, and gives COMELEC power to supervise or regulate media during elections. Jurisprudence provides that broadcasters operate under a franchise and use airwaves that belong to the public, so they may be required to give free airtime as a condition of their franchise.","Here, radio stations broadcast using public airwaves under franchises from the government. Free airtime is a fair condition of that privilege, and the election law authorizes it. Unlike newspapers, the stations do not fully own the medium they use.","Therefore, the order is valid."],"key":"TWIST: print = private property (no free space); broadcast = public airwaves under franchise (free airtime OK)."},{"c":"penera","t":"same","label":"Facebook ads before the campaign","facts":"V files her COC for governor on October 3. The campaign period starts on March 28. In January, she pays for Facebook ads saying “Vote V for Governor!” A rival files a petition to disqualify her for premature campaigning.","q":"Will the petition prosper?","hint":"When does someone who filed a COC become a “candidate”?","alac":["No. The petition will not prosper.","The Constitution gives COMELEC power to enforce election laws. Jurisprudence provides that a person who files a COC is considered a candidate only at the start of the campaign period, so unlawful acts that apply to candidates, like premature campaigning, can be committed only from then on.","Here, V's Facebook ads ran in January, before the campaign period started on March 28. At that time, she was not yet a candidate under the law, even though she had filed her COC. Her ads cannot be premature campaigning.","Therefore, the petition to disqualify her will not prosper."],"key":"COC filed ≠ candidate yet. Candidate status starts with the campaign period."},{"c":"penera","t":"same","label":"Rice bags with the mayor's name","facts":"Incumbent Mayor Q files his COC for re-election in October. In February, before the campaign period, he distributes rice bags printed with “Q Cares” in barangays. His rival seeks his disqualification for premature campaigning.","q":"Is Q liable for premature campaigning?","hint":"Same timing issue.","alac":["No. Q is not liable for premature campaigning.","The Constitution gives COMELEC the power to enforce election laws. Jurisprudence provides that a person who files a COC becomes a candidate only at the start of the campaign period, and that acts done before it are not premature campaigning.","Here, Q distributed the rice bags in February, before the campaign period. Although he had filed his COC, he was not yet a candidate. His acts cannot be treated as premature campaigning.","Therefore, Q is not liable for premature campaigning, though he may still answer under other laws if he used public funds."],"key":"Premature campaigning fails before the campaign period. But other laws, like anti-graft rules on public funds, may still apply."},{"c":"penera","t":"twist","label":"Rally on the eve of the election","facts":"Candidate X holds a big rally on the night before election day, urging people to vote for him. A rival files a complaint for campaigning outside the campaign period. X cites Peñera, saying campaigning is unlawful only during the campaign period.","q":"Is X liable?","hint":"Is X already a candidate by then? Is the eve of the election part of the campaign period?","alac":["Yes. X may be held liable.","The Constitution gives COMELEC the power to enforce election laws. The law provides that the campaign period does not include the day before and the day of the election, and that campaigning outside the campaign period is unlawful. Jurisprudence provides that a person who filed a COC becomes a candidate at the start of the campaign period.","Here, X had already become a candidate when the campaign period started, so election offenses now apply to him. The rally was held the day before the election, which is outside the campaign period. Peñera does not help, because it protects only acts done before one becomes a candidate.","Therefore, X may be held liable for campaigning outside the campaign period."],"key":"TWIST: after the campaign starts, you ARE a candidate. The day before and election day itself are outside the campaign period."},{"c":"seneres","t":"same","label":"District representative's residency","facts":"Candidate D wins a congressional district seat, is proclaimed, takes his oath, and assumes office on June 30. In July, his rival files a petition with COMELEC to annul his proclamation, claiming he did not meet the residency requirement.","q":"Does COMELEC have jurisdiction?","hint":"What three things happened by June 30?","alac":["No. Jurisdiction belongs to the HRET.","The Constitution provides that the House of Representatives Electoral Tribunal is the sole judge of all contests relating to the election, returns, and qualifications of its Members. Jurisprudence provides that once a winning candidate has been proclaimed, has taken the oath, and has assumed office, COMELEC loses jurisdiction and the HRET takes over.","Here, D was proclaimed, took his oath, and assumed office on June 30. The rival's petition was filed in July, after all three steps. Residency is a question of qualification.","Therefore, COMELEC has no jurisdiction, and the rival must file with the HRET."],"key":"Proclamation + oath + assumption → HRET. All three must be present."},{"c":"seneres","t":"same","label":"Senator-elect's citizenship","facts":"Senator E is proclaimed, takes her oath, and assumes office on June 30. In August, a voter files a petition with COMELEC to disqualify her, claiming she is not natural-born.","q":"Where should the petition be filed?","hint":"Same rule, other chamber.","alac":["It should be filed with the Senate Electoral Tribunal.","The Constitution provides that the Senate Electoral Tribunal is the sole judge of all contests relating to the election, returns, and qualifications of its Members. Jurisprudence provides that once a winning candidate has been proclaimed, has taken the oath, and has assumed office, the electoral tribunal has jurisdiction, not COMELEC.","Here, E was proclaimed, took her oath, and assumed office on June 30. The petition was filed in August, after these steps. Citizenship is a qualification issue.","Therefore, COMELEC has no jurisdiction, and the petition belongs to the SET."],"key":"Same rule for the Senate: proclaimed, sworn, assumed → SET."},{"c":"seneres","t":"twist","label":"Proclaimed but not yet assumed","facts":"Party-list P wins a seat, and its first nominee, N, is proclaimed on May 25. On June 10, before N takes office on June 30, a party member files a petition with COMELEC to cancel N's nomination for lack of qualification.","q":"Does COMELEC have jurisdiction?","hint":"Have all three steps happened?","alac":["Yes. COMELEC still has jurisdiction.","The Constitution provides that the House Electoral Tribunal is the sole judge of contests relating to the qualifications of Members of the House. Jurisprudence provides that the tribunal's jurisdiction begins only when the winner has been proclaimed, has taken the oath, and has assumed office; before that, COMELEC keeps jurisdiction.","Here, N was proclaimed on May 25, but the petition was filed on June 10, before N assumed office on June 30. Not all three steps had happened when the petition was filed.","Therefore, COMELEC still has jurisdiction over the petition."],"key":"TWIST: proclamation alone is not enough. Until oath AND assumption, COMELEC keeps jurisdiction."},{"c":"lp","t":"same","label":"Late party registration","facts":"A newly formed party files for registration with COMELEC two months after the registration deadline. It asks to be registered anyway because it has 100,000 members and wants to field candidates in the coming election. COMELEC grants registration.","q":"Was COMELEC's grant proper?","hint":"Is the deadline mandatory?","alac":["No. COMELEC's grant was improper.","The Constitution gives COMELEC the power to register political parties and coalitions. Jurisprudence provides that registration deadlines are mandatory, and that registration requires COMELEC's evaluation, not just the party's existence.","Here, the party filed two months after the deadline. Its large membership does not excuse late filing. Granting registration ignores a mandatory deadline that applies equally to all.","Therefore, COMELEC's grant was improper."],"key":"Deadlines are mandatory. Size or popularity is no excuse for late registration."},{"c":"lp","t":"same","label":"Unregistered coalition seeks accreditation","facts":"Parties A and B, each registered, form Coalition AB but never register it with COMELEC. Before the accreditation deadline, AB asks to be accredited as the dominant majority party, arguing that since A and B are already registered, the coalition need not register.","q":"May COMELEC accredit Coalition AB?","hint":"What must come before accreditation?","alac":["No. COMELEC may not accredit Coalition AB.","The Constitution gives COMELEC the power to register political parties and coalitions. Jurisprudence provides that a coalition must itself be registered before it can be accredited, and that registration requires COMELEC's discretion and evaluation.","Here, AB never registered as a coalition. The fact that A and B are each registered does not make the coalition registered. Accreditation without prior registration skips a required step.","Therefore, COMELEC may not accredit AB."],"key":"Register first, then accredit. Each member being registered is not enough."},{"c":"lp","t":"twist","label":"Coalition registered on time","facts":"Parties C and D form Coalition CD and register it with COMELEC two weeks before the registration deadline. Within the accreditation period, CD asks to be accredited as the dominant minority party. A rival, citing Liberal Party v. COMELEC, opposes.","q":"May COMELEC accredit Coalition CD?","hint":"What was missing in the Liberal Party case?","alac":["Yes. COMELEC may accredit CD.","The Constitution gives COMELEC the power to register political parties and coalitions. Jurisprudence provides that a coalition must register before the deadline and before it can be accredited.","Here, CD registered as a coalition before the deadline and then applied for accreditation within the accreditation period. Both requirements missing in the Liberal Party case are present.","Therefore, COMELEC may evaluate and accredit CD if it meets the criteria."],"key":"TWIST: Liberal Party only bars late or unregistered coalitions. A timely registered coalition may be accredited."},{"c":"galindo","t":"same","label":"COA clerk files certiorari","facts":"COA, after an investigation, dismisses one of its own clerks for falsifying his time records. The clerk files a petition for certiorari with the Supreme Court within 30 days, arguing that decisions of Constitutional Commissions are reviewed by the Supreme Court.","q":"Is certiorari to the Supreme Court the proper remedy?","hint":"Is COA acting as auditor or as employer?","alac":["No. The proper remedy is an appeal to the CSC.","The Constitution provides that decisions of the Constitutional Commissions may be brought to the Supreme Court on certiorari, and makes the CSC the central personnel agency of the government. Jurisprudence provides that when COA disciplines its own employees, it acts as an employer, so the remedy is an appeal to the CSC, not certiorari.","Here, COA dismissed its own clerk for falsifying time records. This is a personnel matter, not an exercise of COA's audit power. The rule on certiorari covers decisions in COA's constitutional functions.","Therefore, the clerk should appeal to the CSC."],"key":"COA as employer → appeal to CSC. COA as auditor → certiorari to the SC."},{"c":"galindo","t":"same","label":"Auditor paid as consultant","facts":"A COA auditor assigned to a state university accepts a part-time “consultancy” with the same university, receiving P20,000 a month. COA finds him guilty of grave misconduct.","q":"Was the auditor properly held liable?","hint":"Why do auditors have to stay independent?","alac":["Yes. The auditor was properly held liable.","The Constitution establishes COA as an independent body that audits government agencies. Jurisprudence provides that COA personnel may not receive benefits or pay from the agencies they audit, because this compromises COA's independence.","Here, the auditor was paid by the very university he was assigned to audit. Calling it a consultancy does not change the fact that he received money from the agency he was checking. His independence was compromised.","Therefore, the auditor was properly held liable."],"key":"Any benefit from an audited agency, whatever it is called, compromises COA's independence."},{"c":"galindo","t":"twist","label":"Disallowance against an agency","facts":"COA, in its audit, disallows P5 million in bonuses paid by a GOCC and holds the GOCC's president personally liable. The president's motion for reconsideration is denied by COA en banc. He files a petition for certiorari with the Supreme Court within 30 days.","q":"Is certiorari to the Supreme Court proper?","hint":"Is COA acting as auditor or as employer here?","alac":["Yes. Certiorari to the Supreme Court is proper.","The Constitution provides that decisions of the Constitutional Commissions may be brought to the Supreme Court on certiorari within 30 days from receipt. Jurisprudence provides that this applies when COA acts in its constitutional function as auditor, unlike when it disciplines its own employees as employer.","Here, COA disallowed bonuses paid by a GOCC in an audit, which is COA's core constitutional function. The person affected is an official of the audited agency, not a COA employee. He filed within 30 days after the denial of his motion for reconsideration.","Therefore, certiorari to the Supreme Court is the proper remedy."],"key":"TWIST: Galindo is only about COA as employer. Disallowances in an audit → certiorari to the SC."},{"c":"bsp","t":"same","label":"GOCC governance fee","facts":"A law requires all GOCCs to pay an annual “governance fee” equal to 1% of their net income. The GOCC Commission bills the BSP. The BSP has no capital stock or members and is the independent central monetary authority.","q":"Must the BSP pay the governance fee?","hint":"Is the BSP a GOCC?","alac":["No. The BSP need not pay the fee.","The Constitution provides for an independent central monetary authority. Jurisprudence provides that a GOCC must be organized as a stock or non-stock corporation, and that the BSP is not a GOCC because it has no capital stock and no members, so laws on GOCCs do not apply to it.","Here, the law covers only GOCCs. The BSP has no capital stock and no members, so it is not a GOCC. It is a government instrumentality with corporate powers that the Constitution makes independent.","Therefore, the BSP need not pay the governance fee."],"key":"No stock + no members = not a GOCC. Laws for GOCCs do not reach the BSP."},{"c":"bsp","t":"same","label":"BSP refuses COA audit","facts":"Relying on the ruling that it is not a GOCC, the BSP refuses to let COA audit its operating expenses, saying it is independent and outside COA's reach.","q":"Is the BSP correct?","hint":"Does being “not a GOCC” and “independent” mean free from audit?","alac":["No. COA may audit the BSP.","The Constitution gives COA the power to audit all government agencies and instrumentalities, including those with original charters. Jurisprudence provides that the BSP is not a GOCC, but it is a government instrumentality, so it remains subject to COA's audit.","Here, the BSP is a government instrumentality with an original charter. Not being a GOCC only means that laws specific to GOCCs do not apply. It does not take the BSP outside COA's constitutional audit power.","Therefore, the BSP is wrong, and COA may audit its expenses."],"key":"Not a GOCC ≠ not audited. COA audits every government instrumentality."},{"c":"bsp","t":"twist","label":"Government-owned stock bank","facts":"A government bank is organized with capital stock, 90% owned by the national government. COA applies the GOCC dividends law to it. The bank, citing BSP v. COA, argues it is also not a GOCC because it serves a public function.","q":"Is the bank covered by the GOCC law?","hint":"Does the bank have capital stock?","alac":["Yes. The bank is covered by the GOCC dividends law.","The Constitution recognizes GOCCs, and the law defines them as agencies organized as stock or non-stock corporations owned or controlled by the government. Jurisprudence provides that the BSP is not a GOCC because it has no stock and no members, but entities organized with capital stock owned by the government are GOCCs.","Here, the bank has capital stock, and the government owns 90% of it. That makes it a GOCC. Serving a public function does not change how it is organized. Unlike the BSP, it has shares of stock.","Therefore, the bank is a GOCC and must comply with the dividends law."],"key":"TWIST: check the structure. Capital stock owned by the government = GOCC, even if it serves the public."},{"c":"miaa","t":"same","label":"Seaport authority's piers","facts":"A city assesses real property tax on the piers, wharves, and port terminals of a national port authority. The port authority has no capital stock and no members and was created by its own charter.","q":"Are the piers and terminals taxable?","hint":"Is the port authority a GOCC, and who owns the piers?","alac":["No. They are exempt from real property tax.","The Constitution recognizes the power of local governments to tax, subject to limits set by law. Jurisprudence provides that a government instrumentality with corporate powers, which has no capital stock and no members, is not a GOCC, and that its property used for public purposes belongs to the State and is exempt from real property tax.","Here, the port authority has no capital stock and no members, so it is an instrumentality, not a GOCC. Piers, wharves, and port terminals are used by the public and are owned by the Republic. They are property of public dominion.","Therefore, the city cannot tax them."],"key":"Instrumentality + property of public dominion → exempt. Same reasoning as airports."},{"c":"miaa","t":"same","label":"Runway put up for auction","facts":"After MIAA fails to pay the real property tax it was assessed, the city announces a public auction of an airport runway to collect the tax.","q":"May the city sell the runway?","hint":"Can property of public dominion be sold?","alac":["No. The city may not sell the runway.","The Constitution recognizes the taxing power of local governments within limits. Jurisprudence provides that airport lands and buildings, such as runways and terminals, are property of public dominion owned by the Republic, exempt from real property tax, and cannot be sold at public auction.","Here, a runway is used by the public for air travel and is owned by the Republic. Since it is exempt, there is no valid tax to collect. And since it is property of public dominion, it cannot be sold to private bidders.","Therefore, the city may not auction the runway."],"key":"Public dominion property is outside commerce: no tax and no auction."},{"c":"miaa","t":"twist","label":"Government-owned hotel corporation","facts":"A government-owned corporation with capital stock runs a hotel and resort. The city assesses real property tax on the hotel land and buildings. The corporation, citing MIAA v. City of Pasay, claims exemption because it is government-owned.","q":"Are the hotel properties taxable?","hint":"Does this entity have stock? What are the properties used for?","alac":["Yes. The hotel properties are taxable.","The Constitution recognizes the taxing power of local governments within limits set by law. The law withdraws tax exemptions of GOCCs. Jurisprudence provides that MIAA is exempt because it is an instrumentality with no stock and its airport property is of public dominion; an entity with capital stock is a GOCC.","Here, the corporation has capital stock, so it is a GOCC, not an instrumentality. The hotel and resort are used for commercial business, not for public use like a runway. MIAA's reasoning does not apply.","Therefore, the hotel properties are taxable."],"key":"TWIST: two checks: (1) stock or none? (2) public use or business? A stock GOCC running a business is taxable."},{"c":"chrea","t":"same","label":"CHR creates new positions","facts":"Invoking its independence, the CHR uses its savings to create 20 new plantilla positions without DBM approval. The DBM refuses to recognize them.","q":"Is the CHR's action valid?","hint":"Is the CHR one of the bodies with fiscal autonomy?","alac":["No. The CHR's action is invalid.","The Constitution grants fiscal autonomy only to the Judiciary, the Constitutional Commissions, and the Ombudsman. Jurisprudence provides that the CHR, though independent, does not have fiscal autonomy, so it must comply with the Salary Standardization Law and obtain DBM approval for changes to positions.","Here, the CHR created new positions on its own, without DBM approval. Being independent is not the same as having fiscal autonomy. The CHR is not among the bodies the Constitution lists.","Therefore, the CHR's action is invalid."],"key":"Independent ≠ fiscally autonomous. Fiscal autonomy is only for J-C-O."},{"c":"chrea","t":"same","label":"Agency called “independent” by its charter","facts":"A law creates a “National Anti-Poverty Commission” and describes it in its charter as “independent.” The Commission reclassifies its directors to higher salary grades without DBM approval, citing its independence and fiscal autonomy.","q":"Is the reclassification valid?","hint":"Where does fiscal autonomy come from?","alac":["No. The reclassification is invalid.","The Constitution grants fiscal autonomy only to the Judiciary, the Constitutional Commissions, and the Ombudsman. Jurisprudence provides that other bodies, even if described as independent, do not have fiscal autonomy and must get DBM approval to change positions and salary grades.","Here, the Commission was created by law, not by the Constitution, and is not among the three. A law calling it independent does not give it the fiscal autonomy the Constitution reserves for others.","Therefore, the reclassification without DBM approval is invalid."],"key":"A charter's label of “independent” cannot add a body to the J-C-O list."},{"c":"chrea","t":"twist","label":"COMELEC uses its savings","facts":"Near year-end, the COMELEC Chairman uses savings from COMELEC's own appropriations to augment its item for voter education, as the GAA allows. The DBM objects that COMELEC needs its approval.","q":"Is COMELEC's augmentation valid?","hint":"Is COMELEC one of the J-C-O? What does the Constitution allow heads of Commissions to do with savings?","alac":["Yes. COMELEC's augmentation is valid.","The Constitution grants fiscal autonomy to the Constitutional Commissions, and allows the heads of Constitutional Commissions, when authorized by law, to augment any item in the GAA for their own offices from savings in their other items. Jurisprudence provides that fiscal autonomy gives these bodies full flexibility to use their resources.","Here, COMELEC is a Constitutional Commission with fiscal autonomy. The Chairman used savings from COMELEC's own items to augment another COMELEC item, as the GAA allows. It did not move funds to other agencies.","Therefore, the augmentation is valid without DBM approval."],"key":"TWIST: COMELEC is one of the J-C-O. Augmenting its own items from its own savings, when the GAA authorizes it, is allowed."}]`);
